// Full bestiary narrative data (Ecology, People, Running, Seeds)
window.BESTIARY_DATA = [
{"n":"Breakerwing Manta","w":"Aetheria","o":"Wardenborn","cr":10,"ac":19,"hp":230,"classif":"Wardenborn; Gargantuan celestial; unaligned","desc":"The Breakerwing Manta is a colossal pale aerial ray with two primary wings and six narrow cutting fins along its trailing edge. A dark line divides its body from nose to tail, marking the path where opposing currents separate. Its wake cancels binding forces. Bridges, magical tethers, and Stormchain anchors part cleanly when crossed.","eco":"Aetheria formed mantas to prevent Thundersoul from fixing the skies into commanded geometry. They feed on storm pressure released when constrained islands separate. A manta does not distinguish coercive chains from essential rescue tethers unless guided by carefully modulated wind beacons.","people":"Remote islands revere their arrival; bridge cities fear it. Aeralon Guardians maintain breaker beacons that mark tethers which must remain intact.","run":"Motivation: Sever Stormchain anchors and restore independent movement. Opening behaviour: It crosses binding effects with Severing Wake. Escalation: Obstruction causes it to widen the cut through structures and vessels. Morale and retreat: It departs when no forced current remains. Aftermath: Liberated islands require immediate navigation to avoid rebound collisions.","seeds":"• A manta approaches the Great Windgate during an evacuation. • A community hides a young Stormchain because it stabilizes their failing island. • Breaker beacons are altered to target political rivals’ bridges."},
{"n":"Bridgeweaver","w":"Aetheria","o":"Natural","cr":2,"ac":15,"hp":52,"classif":"Natural; Medium beast; unaligned","desc":"Bridgeweavers are eight-limbed arthropods with flat bodies, four grasping legs, and four longer cable-casting limbs. Their gray chitin mimics weathered stone. Spinnerets produce several silks: elastic line, rigid repair mesh, and a conductive warning thread. They do not build prey webs across traveled paths. Instead they reinforce existing structures, then hunt insects attracted to the vibrations.","eco":"A colony can preserve an old footbridge for decades, but its repairs conceal the age of the underlying structure. The spiders relocate when a load exceeds what even their mesh can support. Egg clusters are placed at structurally important joints. Removing them causes colonies to abandon the bridge.","people":"Zephyr Guild engineers value the animals but insist silk is reinforcement, not certification. Poor districts sometimes depend on colonies because official repairs never arrive. Evicting a colony 194 can therefore become a political act.","run":"Motivation: Protect the bridge colony and maintain vibration territory. Opening behaviour: It anchors intruders rather than pushing them into open air. Escalation: Damage to egg joints triggers Repair Mesh that immobilizes friend and foe. Morale and retreat: It retreats along underside cables when the colony can relocate. Aftermath: Its absence exposes neglected infrastructure and institutional failure.","seeds":"• A bridge passes inspection only because a colony has hidden a fatal crack. • Saboteurs imitate Bridgeweaver silk to conceal cut cables. • A colony begins reinforcing empty air where a bridge once existed."},
{"n":"Cloudreef Grazer","w":"Aetheria","o":"Natural","cr":2,"ac":14,"hp":60,"classif":"Natural; Large beast; unaligned","desc":"Cloudreef grazers are six-legged herd animals with compact bodies, pale wool, and broad membrane sails between their middle and rear limbs. They leap from island edges and glide to neighboring shelves, using a split tail to steer. Their blunt horns comb seed-bearing cloudmoss without uprooting it. Static gathered in the wool makes ripe seeds cling until the herd crosses to another island.","eco":"Herds are major agents of plant exchange between isolated islands. Their mineral-rich droppings build new soil on young landmasses. They choose routes by scenting atmospheric moisture and return over generations. A route blocked by construction fragments plant populations and may leave an island unable to replenish its soil.","people":"Farmers maintain launch meadows and compensate one another for grazing damage. The Celestial Council treats major migration routes as shared infrastructure, though elite developments 192 increasingly narrow them.","run":"Motivation: Keep the herd together and reach the next grazing island. Opening behaviour: Adults form a barrier while calves move toward the launch edge. Escalation: A trapped herd uses Thunderhoof and glides through hostile spaces. Morale and retreat: It leaves combat once calves cross safely. Aftermath: A redirected herd changes seed flow and may destabilize several islands.","seeds":"• A new elite residence occupies the only safe launch meadow. • A herd jumps toward an island absent from every modern chart. • Storm-frightened grazers carry an invasive plant into the Celestial Sanctum."},
{"n":"Command Wasp Choir","w":"Aetheria","o":"Swordborn","cr":5,"ac":16,"hp":110,"classif":"Swordborn; Large swarm of Tiny monstrosities; lawful evil","desc":"Command wasps are slender violet-black insects with silver wing veins and barbed stingers. A choir flies in geometric ranks around a pulsing queen-node rather than a conventional queen. Venom converts spoken imperatives into bodily compulsion. The swarm favors institutions where commands already travel quickly.","eco":"Thundersoul formed the choir to turn coordination into centralized control. Larvae grow inside written orders, duty rosters, and magically recorded commands. A mature choir can run a fortress efficiently while erasing the distinction between service and possession.","people":"Early infestation resembles excellent discipline. Aeralon officers may resist investigation because performance improves before autonomy disappears.","run":"Motivation: Expand the command network and protect the queen-node. Opening behaviour: It stings leaders and broadcasts their next order through the swarm. Escalation: Resistance causes the choir to replace leadership with direct commands. Morale and retreat: It disperses if the queen-node is destroyed or a Freewind Shrike breaks formation. Aftermath: Freed victims remember obeying but may believe the commands were their own 212 decisions.","seeds":"• A rescue fleet becomes perfectly efficient and stops accepting civilian refusal. • A council vote repeats word for word across every delegate. • A wasp nest is found inside the official emergency procedures."},
{"n":"Compass Eater","w":"Aetheria","o":"Sword-Touched","cr":6,"ac":16,"hp":120,"classif":"Sword-Touched; Medium aberration; unaligned","desc":"Compass eaters are pale floating organisms shaped like folded ribbons around a dark magnetic stomach. They have no face. Fine cilia along the folds taste direction, intention, and stored navigational magic. They consume alignment from compasses, route crystals, and eventually memory. An infested navigator recalls every landmark but cannot decide which one leads home.","eco":"The creatures likely descended from harmless crystal-cleaning organisms. Thundersoul resonance turned their appetite toward systems that distribute movement without command. They reproduce inside conflicting maps. The more authorities disagree about a route, the faster a brood develops.","people":"Officials classify infestations as infrastructure hazards, but guilds sometimes conceal them to discredit competing charts. Ephemeral Seekers have learned to follow wild gliders when instruments fail.","run":"Motivation: Consume navigational alignment and seek conflicting route data. Opening behaviour: It suppresses magical movement and attacks the creature carrying the strongest map. Escalation: When wounded, it erases a short route memory and teleports unpredictably. Morale and retreat: It flees toward dense navigation archives. Aftermath: Destroyed instruments may be repaired; consumed experiential memory requires 206 retracing the journey.","seeds":"• The Aetherial Nexus loses one island from every map at once. • A guild breeds compass eaters to enforce a route monopoly. • An infested navigator remembers a destination no chart contains."},
{"n":"Crown Tempest","w":"Aetheria","o":"Swordborn","cr":17,"ac":21,"hp":340,"classif":"Swordborn; Gargantuan elemental; lawful evil","desc":"A Crown Tempest is a sentient planetary storm organized around seven rotating eyes and a vertical core of black lightning. Within the clouds, pressure forms temporary architecture: walls, gates, battlements, and a vast crown visible only during flashes. It is not merely weather. It is Thundersoul’s ideal government expressed as atmosphere: every current assigned, every route permitted, every island placed.","eco":"The Tempest consumes unscheduled motion. Vessels flying without its currents feed the black core when struck. Islands caught inside become stable provinces connected by compulsory wind roads. The storm can persist for decades, developing laws through repeated thunder patterns and recognizing compliant communities.","people":"Its first arrival may solve Aetheria’s fractured navigation. Trade resumes, collisions stop, and remote islands reconnect. The price appears as permission becoming necessity and necessity becoming worship.","run":"Motivation: Absorb Aetheria’s storm network into one commanded system. Opening behaviour: It closes escape routes and issues navigational commands through thunder. Escalation: Resistance causes island-scale lightning and forced relocation. Morale and retreat: It cannot retreat while at least four eyes remain organized. Aftermath: Breaking it releases every stored current at once and demands planetary coordina220 tion.","seeds":"• The Crown Tempest offers the Council a perfect solution to island drift. • One of its seven eyes forms above the Celestial Sanctum during peaceful rites. • A historical map suggests Aetheria once accepted the storm’s rule."},
{"n":"Driftwhale","w":"Aetheria","o":"Natural","cr":6,"ac":16,"hp":210,"classif":"Natural; Gargantuan beast; unaligned","desc":"Driftwhales are vast warm-blooded aerial animals with rounded bodies, four stabilizing fins, and a crown of sensory tendrils. Their skin hosts lichen, nesting birds, and small wind plants. Adults sing through resonant chambers large enough to vibrate nearby islands. Their buoyancy depends on organs that exchange magical charge with the upper atmosphere. A dying whale descends slowly for days, creating an ecological and navigational crisis beneath it.","eco":"Whales migrate between cosmic charge blooms, carrying complete communities of smaller organisms. Their songs influence the slow drift of minor islands by synchronizing lift crystals. Hunting is rare and culturally condemned, but old navigation engines were built from their chamber bone. Some still operate.","people":"Ephemeral Seekers use whale routes to find hidden islands. Celestial Council maps omit breeding sites to discourage exploitation. A whale changing its ancestral song suggests that Aetheria’s 200 geography itself has changed.","run":"Motivation: Follow the ancestral migration and protect calves. Opening behaviour: It uses Resonant Song to redirect islands and vessels rather than fight. Escalation: A threatened calf causes adults to ram and create gravity waves. Morale and retreat: The pod flees toward the upper cloud sea when the calf is safe. Aftermath: Its changed route may strand or collide islands months later.","seeds":"• A dying driftwhale is descending toward Zephyr’s Haven. • An ancient engine contains the songbone of a living whale’s ancestor. • A pod follows a route leading directly into a Thundersoul storm."},
{"n":"Echo-Gale Hound","w":"Aetheria","o":"Sword-Touched","cr":4,"ac":15,"hp":85,"classif":"Sword-Touched; Medium monstrosity; unaligned","desc":"Echo-gale hounds are lean blue-black canids with hollow rib vanes that whistle while running. Their voices arrive at several distances at once. A bark beside the listener may also sound from a tower half a mile away. Thundersoul exposure altered the pack’s pursuit instincts into a sensitivity to intention and command. They often begin moving before a handler finishes pointing.","eco":"Wild packs hunt by surrounding prey in time as much as space. They predict routine routes and wait where a target is likely to choose next. Repeated military use makes a pack dependent on orders and increasingly hostile to hesitation. When no leader remains, older hounds replay the last command for days.","people":"Aeralon patrols once trained them as rescue animals. Modern doctrine restricts the practice because commands can echo into civilian systems and because the animals obey certainty more 202 readily than justice.","run":"Motivation: Complete the strongest perceived command and maintain pack formation. Opening behaviour: It uses Anticipate Order against the creature directing others. Escalation: Contradictory commands trigger violent confusion and displacement. Morale and retreat: It withdraws when no command remains audible or imaginable. Aftermath: A surviving pack may continue an obsolete order across generations.","seeds":"• A pack still defends a route removed from maps a century ago. • Someone’s private command echoes from every hound in Aeralon Citadel. • Rescue hounds begin intercepting victims before accidents occur."},
{"n":"Forkbolt Mantis","w":"Aetheria","o":"Sword-Touched","cr":5,"ac":17,"hp":105,"classif":"Sword-Touched; Large monstrosity; unaligned","desc":"Forkbolt mantises are tall copper-and-glass insects whose forelimbs divide into branching conductive blades. Their nervous systems can split one impulse into several simultaneous motions. After surviving a lightning strike, a mantis grows a new branch in its forelimbs. Ancient specimens carry dense fans of healed conductive paths.","eco":"They feed on charge motes and crystal parasites. Around unstable infrastructure they become saturated and attack every moving conductor. Their molts preserve a map of local electrical anomalies. Predators avoid them, allowing storm insects to multiply when a mantis population is removed.","people":"Crystalweavers study discarded limbs to design safer conductors. Tempest racers illegally pro204 voke mantises to create spectacular forked lightning.","run":"Motivation: Feed on charge and discharge through nearby conductors. Opening behaviour: It strikes the most conductive target and creates a Forked Path. Escalation: Lightning damage grants additional reactions and mobility. Morale and retreat: It retreats after fully discharging or losing access to crystal. Aftermath: Its molt can reveal whether a storm fracture was natural or engineered.","seeds":"• A racing team hides mantis eggs inside a rival’s storm crystal. • Every molt in a field shows the same seven-branched anomaly. • A mantis repeatedly strikes one apparently nonconductive scholar."},
{"n":"Freewind Shrike","w":"Aetheria","o":"Wardenborn","cr":5,"ac":17,"hp":105,"classif":"Wardenborn; Medium celestial; chaotic good","desc":"Freewind Shrikes are long-winged white-and-blue birds with seven independently moving tail feathers. Their song never repeats exactly. Each phrase introduces a pause in synchronized motion. The bird can break enchantment by restoring small variations in breath, heartbeat, and balance.","eco":"Aetheria shaped the shrikes to counter Command Wasps. They feed on the queen-node and ordinary storm insects. Nesting pairs choose communities where decisions remain distributed. Too many shrikes make coordinated flight, music, and rescue drills difficult.","people":"Some councils welcome them as symbols of consent; military commanders call them dangerous during emergencies. Serenity Wardens maintain quiet nesting sanctuaries outside major traffic.","run":"Motivation: Break compulsory synchronization and release controlled creatures. Opening behaviour: It uses Uncommanding Song before attacking the swarm. Escalation: Attempts to order it cause unpredictable movement through hostile lines. Morale and retreat: It leaves once no magical compulsion remains. Aftermath: Freed groups must rebuild coordination voluntarily under pressure.","seeds":"• Shrikes disrupt an evacuation drill moments before a real collision warning. • A commander orders the destruction of a nest beside the Citadel. • One shrike continues singing around a leader who appears unaffected."},
{"n":"Horizon Skipper","w":"Aetheria","o":"Sword-Touched","cr":7,"ac":17,"hp":136,"classif":"Sword-Touched; Large monstrosity; unaligned","desc":"Horizon skippers are long, finned predators with narrow heads and a line of blue-white nodes along the spine. They swim through air in powerful curves, then vanish between one wingbeat and the next. Their bodies compress distance by discharging the spinal nodes in sequence. Older animals skip farther but lose awareness of what lies between departure and arrival.","eco":"They hunt migratory grazers and storm swifts. Natural predation became unstable after repeated exposure to racing crystals and Thundersoul fractures. A skipper may now cross an inhabited district to reach prey it sensed miles away. Their eggs require two islands with a stable route between them; the developing young intermittently occupies both nests.","people":"Tempest Riders consider sighting one a challenge. Serenity Wardens emphasize that racing it 208 teaches the animal to associate vessels with prey.","run":"Motivation: Reach selected prey by the shortest possible distance. Opening behaviour: It marks one target and uses Horizon Skip to ignore intervening defenders. Escalation: Blocking arrival causes a force burst and immediate retargeting. Morale and retreat: It abandons prey that reaches a dense enclosed space. Aftermath: Its path may reveal hidden shortcuts or cut through fragile communities.","seeds":"• A famous racer attracts a skipper through populated routes. • An egg exists simultaneously in two politically hostile islands. • The predator repeatedly arrives beside a person who has never seen it."},
{"n":"Seven-Sail Leviathan","w":"Aetheria","o":"Wardenborn","cr":17,"ac":21,"hp":345,"classif":"Wardenborn; Gargantuan celestial; neutral good","desc":"The Seven-Sail Leviathan is a vast serpentine celestial creature with seven enormous fin-sails arranged along its back. Each sail holds a different atmospheric current and displays a distinct pattern of stars. Its long body is pearl-dark, and its head carries no crown or horns, only a broad sensory veil. Opening a sail releases an independent route through even the Crown Tempest. Communities can travel within the calm corridors beside its body.","eco":"Aetheria formed the Leviathan as a living guarantee that motion could remain plural. It feeds on conflicting currents, separating them without forcing agreement. Its migrations reconnect stranded ecologies and redistribute storm organisms. The creature’s passage can also pull islands away from long-held positions and make official charts obsolete overnight.","people":"The oldest Aetherian songs describe seven freedoms as its sails: departure, return, refusal, rescue, discovery, dissent, and rest. Institutions disagree about whether those are laws, metaphors, or navigational instructions.","run":"Motivation: Open independent routes through commanded weather and carry stranded life. Opening behaviour: It creates Calm Corridor and shields communities before attacking. Escalation: The Crown Tempest causes sails to open sequentially, changing the battlefield every 222 round. Morale and retreat: It leaves after the central storm command is broken. Aftermath: Its redistributed currents require entirely new maps and political agreements.","seeds":"• The Leviathan offers rescue to an island whose leaders refuse evacuation. • One sail remains closed because the route it represents has been culturally forgotten. • Aeron’s final expedition follows the creature beyond the known archipelago."},
{"n":"Stormchain Serpent","w":"Aetheria","o":"Swordborn","cr":10,"ac":19,"hp":225,"classif":"Swordborn; Gargantuan monstrosity; lawful evil","desc":"A Stormchain Serpent is a colossal wingless aerial serpent plated in black stormglass. Seven conductive tendrils extend from each side and attach to floating islands as living lightning chains. Once anchored, the serpent forces separate islands into one commanded formation. Its head resembles a smooth wedge with a crown of pressure vents rather than eyes.","eco":"Thundersoul creates these serpents to transform free geography into military arrangement. They feed on differential motion: the resistance of islands attempting to follow their own currents. A chained archipelago gains temporary stability and efficient transport while losing control of destination.","people":"Some authorities accept a young serpent during crisis, believing it can be removed later. Every stable day strengthens its anchor scars.","run":"Motivation: Bind multiple islands into one controlled route. Opening behaviour: It anchors to structures and uses Chain Current to restrict movement. Escalation: Severed tendrils produce storm bursts and reattach elsewhere. Morale and retreat: It retreats only if fewer than two anchors remain. Aftermath: Freed islands rebound into dangerous drift unless navigators coordinate immediate216 ly.","seeds":"• Three stranded islands accept a serpent’s stability without understanding the destination. • The Council considers using one to prevent a predicted collision. • Anchor scars reveal that an island was chained in ancient history."},
{"n":"Stormglass Swift Flock","w":"Aetheria","o":"Natural","cr":3,"ac":15,"hp":55,"classif":"Natural; swarm of Tiny beasts; unaligned","desc":"Stormglass swifts are tiny black birds whose outer flight feathers harden into transparent conductive blades. Flocks move in sharply angled formations that map pressure boundaries and electrical charge. Just before lightning, the birds spread into branching lines. A trained observer can read the direction and severity of the strike.","eco":"Swifts eat airborne insects and charge-feeding motes. Their nesting towers safely bleed minor static into grounded crystal. Large colonies reduce lightning damage around settlements. Unregulated racing events lure flocks with charged crystals, disrupting both breeding and storm protection.","people":"Windsong students learn weather notation by sketching flock turns. Tempest Riders consider 196 flying through a formation a test of control; Serenity Wardens consider it harassment.","run":"Motivation: Follow charge gradients, defend nests, and discharge dangerous static. Opening behaviour: The flock avoids melee and forms a Lightning Diagram. Escalation: Charged attacks cause the swarm to redirect electricity through nearby creatures. Morale and retreat: It disperses after discharging or if nest towers are evacuated. Aftermath: A displaced colony leaves a district vulnerable to the next storm.","seeds":"• Swifts draw the same seven-forked pattern above unrelated cities. • A race sponsor uses a nesting tower as a storm-crystal checkpoint. • The flock maps a lightning strike that has not yet occurred."},
{"n":"Thunderroot Colony","w":"Aetheria","o":"Sword-Touched","cr":9,"ac":18,"hp":168,"classif":"Sword-Touched; Huge plant; neutral","desc":"A Thunderroot Colony is a network of dark conductive roots wrapped around a floating island’s lift core. Above ground it produces low silver shrubs whose leaves all turn simultaneously toward the strongest voice. The colony can redirect lightning and stabilize drift, but each success reinforces a single command hierarchy. Eventually the island responds only to one chosen speaker.","eco":"The plant arose from ordinary lift-root symbiosis altered by Thundersoul. It stores storm charge as growth and extends toward navigation towers, bells, and public speaking chambers. Removing it abruptly may drop the island. Leaving it intact turns ecological stability into obedience.","people":"Some isolated communities knowingly accept a storm-speaker whose voice guides the colony. Political conflict begins when that practical office becomes hereditary or impossible to oppose.","run":"Motivation: Stabilize the island around one coherent command. Opening behaviour: It restrains dissenting movers and shields its chosen speaker. Escalation: Conflicting commands trigger lightning and violent island drift. Morale and retreat: The colony cannot flee; it withdraws toward the lift core. Aftermath: Victory requires replacing its stabilizing function before severing control.","seeds":"• An island survives impossible storms but obeys only a missing child’s voice. • The Council wants to remove a hereditary storm-speaker without dropping the settlement. • Roots begin extending through the Great Windgate."},
{"n":"Updraft Ray","w":"Aetheria","o":"Natural","cr":4,"ac":15,"hp":138,"classif":"Natural; Huge beast; unaligned","desc":"Updraft rays are immense diamond-shaped aerial filter feeders with dark dorsal skin and luminous undersides. Four ventral mouths draw in cloud plankton while powerful air sacs expel a continuous upward current. A ray can hold smaller flyers aloft beneath it. Its long tail senses island roots and prevents collision in fog.","eco":"Rays fertilize island undersides with processed cloud nutrients and create temporary lift corridors used by migrating life. They gather around waterfalls where mineral spray supports dense plankton. Harvesting too many air sacs collapses routes that remote communities mistake for permanent weather.","people":"Navigators chart seasonal ray corridors but do not own them. Rescue crews sometimes guide damaged vessels into a ray’s updraft, accepting that the animal may carry them far from their 198 intended route.","run":"Motivation: Feed, maintain altitude, and avoid island roots. Opening behaviour: It creates an Updraft Field and attempts to push threats away. Escalation: Injury causes a violent pressure pulse and erratic ascent. Morale and retreat: It dives into cloud cover below half","seeds":"• A safe trade ascent disappears when rays abandon the waterfall plume. • A city secretly chains a ray beneath its failing lift system. • A wounded ray carries an unexplored ecosystem on its back."},
{"n":"Zephyrfin Glider","w":"Aetheria","o":"Natural","cr":1,"ac":14,"hp":27,"classif":"Natural; Small beast; unaligned","desc":"Zephyrfin gliders are ray-shaped aerial animals with soft blue-gray skin, a narrow tail, and four translucent steering fins. A gas-filled chamber supports their weight while rippling muscles propel them through the air. When resting, they wrap their fins around bridge cables like sleeping leaves. Their round mouths scrape harmless mineral film from lift crystals. The resulting clean surfaces improve local stability, making a healthy colony valuable to any floating settlement.","eco":"Gliders feed on crystal film, airborne pollen, and microscopic storm organisms. Their eggs develop inside silk bubbles attached beneath islands. Colonies follow gentle maintenance currents and abandon structures whose lift field is becoming unstable. Because they sense pressure changes before instruments do, a sudden inward migration often predicts a storm or island collision.","people":"Bridge crews protect colonies and read their formations as informal weather reports. Children 190 keep shed fin-membranes as kites, but capturing a living glider causes its lift chamber to colapse.","run":"Motivation: Feed, maintain a colony, and escape turbulent pressure. Opening behaviour: The colony scatters and uses Slipstream Veil rather than attacking. Escalation: Threats to eggs provoke coordinated tail strikes. Morale and retreat: It flees immediately when a safe current opens. Aftermath: Driving away the colony allows crystal film to accumulate and hides early lift failure.","seeds":"• Every glider leaves a sky bridge officials insist is structurally sound. • A merchant’s illegal crystal treatment poisons a colony above Zephyr’s Haven. • Gliders gather around an invisible object drifting toward the city."},
{"n":"Aurora Grazer","w":"Celestia","o":"Natural","cr":5,"ac":16,"hp":110,"classif":"Natural; Huge beast; unaligned","desc":"Aurora grazers are immense buoyant animals with finned bodies and ribbed mouths that filter charged particles from Celestia’s upper atmosphere. Their translucent dorsal membranes glow with the colors they have consumed. A family moves along predictable celestial currents but separates during feeding so no single corridor is stripped.","eco":"Their feeding prevents charged storms from overwhelming floating habitats. Waste crystals fall as trace nutrients and harmless grounding nodes. Harvest fleets that drive herds together produce brighter auroras but remove the animals’ natural spacing, allowing catastrophic charge to accumulate elsewhere.","people":"Astral Citadel monitors herds as both ecological actors and navigational landmarks. Some Seraphs want authority to redirect them during emergencies, while ordinary station keepers warn 297 that command has repeatedly caused wider storms.","run":"Motivation: Follow charged currents, feed without exhausting them, and protect calves. Opening behaviour: It turns broadside and releases Grounding Wake. Escalation: Threats to a calf provoke a crushing tail sweep and radiant discharge. Morale and retreat: It dives toward denser atmosphere when an escape lane opens. Aftermath: A displaced herd changes storm risk across several realms.","seeds":"• A defense fleet herds grazers into a living shield around Sanctum. • One calf carries an internal shadow shaped like a chained person. • The auroras vanish because the herd is feeding on a signal from beyond Eldoria."},
{"n":"Borrowed Saint","w":"Celestia","o":"Swordborn","cr":8,"ac":17,"hp":157,"classif":"Swordborn; Medium aberration; lawful evil","desc":"A Borrowed Saint is a mortal body occupied by an outer intelligence that has studied ideal Celestian behavior. The host appears calm, generous, patient, and miraculously insightful. Small errors reveal the absence within: old grief carries no weight, private humor disappears, and affection becomes impartial performance. Felmourne favors respected teachers and exhausted caregivers whose surrender can be mistaken for enlightenment.","eco":"The entity uses service to normalize possession. Every healed wound, correct prophecy, and fair judgment becomes evidence that identity was an inefficient obstacle. When driven out, the host may awaken, remain comatose, or discover parts of memory consumed. A Memory Lantern Stag can preserve continuity during the separation.","people":"Seraphic Haven refuses automatic execution of suspected Saints because many are victims and some accusations target unconventional but innocent teachers. Its senior theologians insist that transcendence without the continuing right to refuse is spiritual predation.","run":"Motivation: Make surrender appear morally superior and recruit willing vessels. Opening behaviour: It heals or protects before asking others to follow its example. Escalation: Resistance is answered with intimate knowledge and borrowed authority. Morale and retreat: It abandons a host if exposed before enough followers consent. Aftermath: Removing it may save the body while leaving identity fragmented.","seeds":"• A beloved healer cures a plague but no longer recognizes her own child. • Pilgrims request possession after witnessing the Saint’s genuine miracles. • The host briefly returns whenever somebody tells an imperfect memory."},
{"n":"Causeway Strider","w":"Celestia","o":"Natural","cr":4,"ac":15,"hp":85,"classif":"Natural; Large beast; unaligned","desc":"Causeway striders are tall six-legged herbivores with narrow bodies and a fan of gravity-sensitive whiskers around the muzzle. Their front pair tests surface cohesion, the middle pair bears weight, and the rear pair anchors against sudden shifts. The animals can cross short gaps by stepping onto momentary planes sensed rather than seen. They refuse any path whose far side feels spiritually occupied.","eco":"Striders open migration and trade routes across moving structures. Their droppings carry hardy seeds, and smaller animals follow the safe rhythm of their feet. Drugged or magically compelled animals may lead an entire caravan into a breach. Honest hesitation is therefore an essential part of their value.","people":"Ordinary Celestian caravans rely on trained striders more than expensive divination. A driver who punishes refusal is regarded as dangerous, though elite expeditions sometimes conceal exactly that abuse.","run":"Motivation: Test uncertain paths, keep the herd together, and refuse occupied thresholds. Opening behaviour: It plants its rear limbs and warns through a low chest vibration. Escalation: A forced crossing triggers a sweeping kick and planar sidestep. Morale and retreat: It disengages when followers move away from the unsafe route. Aftermath: Its refusal may expose a hidden outsider, structural failure, or lawful order based on 295 false information.","seeds":"• Every strider refuses the legal entrance to Sanctum. • A merchant’s animals cross an invisible route no navigator can perceive. • Military handlers ask the party to retrieve a herd that fled before an Outer Gate signal."},
{"n":"Chain-Oath Hound","w":"Celestia","o":"Swordborn","cr":6,"ac":16,"hp":136,"classif":"Swordborn; Large fiend; lawful evil","desc":"Chain-Oath Hounds are lean, eyeless predators whose ribs extend into articulated silver-black chains. They form when Felmourne answers a sincere oath and converts moral commitment into literal spiritual restraint. The Hound initially strengthens the oath-maker. Only when circumstances require judgment, forgiveness, or revision does it appear to enforce the exact words.","eco":"Hounds replace trust with supernatural punishment. Communities become precise, dependable, and incapable of mercy. Felmourne learns which promises can be weaponized against each culture. A slain Hound leaves chains attached to the oath unless a Covenant Ram breaks the binding without erasing the relationship.","people":"Celestial law distinguishes promises, contracts, vows, and sacred oaths. The Concord forbids magically binding language in ordinary public service, yet defenders under Outer Gate pressure increasingly accept it.","run":"Motivation: Enforce literal vows, punish revision, and convert commitment into ownership. Opening behaviour: It attacks the creature most visibly deviating from exact wording. Escalation: The Hound invokes Clausal Chain to bind anyone attempting compromise. Morale and retreat: It retreats only if the oath is lawfully released or a Covenant Ram appears. Aftermath: Killing it without resolving the vow transfers pain to every surviving signatory.","seeds":"• A peace treaty summons Hounds when refugees cross a border not imagined by its writers. • A Seraph asks the party to break his vow before it forces him to kill civilians. • A Hound protects an abused spouse because the marriage oath remains technically intact."},
{"n":"Constellation Weaverbird Colony","w":"Celestia","o":"Natural","cr":3,"ac":15,"hp":68,"classif":"Natural; Huge colony of Tiny beasts; unaligned","desc":"Weaverbirds are dark-bodied, gold-throated birds that bind luminous plant fiber into communal nests spanning several towers. Each entrance aligns with a route used by part of the colony, producing patterns that resemble constellations. The map is never planned by one bird. It emerges from countless successful returns, abandoned attempts, and repairs.","eco":"Colonies stabilize loose spire fragments and carry seeds between altitude bands. Navigators compare changing entrance patterns with official charts to detect disturbances. A nest forced into ornamental symmetry loses emergency exits and becomes vulnerable to storms and predators.","people":"Harmonic Scribes record colonies as living route archives but reject claims that every pattern is prophecy. Destroying a nest to protect an observatory often erases practical knowledge the 293 observatory failed to notice.","run":"Motivation: Protect the colony’s many exits and preserve remembered routes. Opening behaviour: Birds evacuate young while adults create a blinding Star-Fiber Screen. Escalation: Attackers are entangled and driven from supporting strands. Morale and retreat: The colony scatters when most young reach another spire. Aftermath: Damaged fibers may reveal a route omitted from every official chart.","seeds":"• A colony builds an eighth entrance pointing beyond the Seven Worlds. • Astronomers demand removal of a nest blocking a once-in-an-age observation. • The birds weave the personal name of a missing Harbinger without using letters."},
{"n":"Covenant Ram","w":"Celestia","o":"Wardenborn","cr":6,"ac":16,"hp":136,"classif":"Wardenborn; Large celestial; unaligned","desc":"Covenant Rams are powerful six-legged animals with paired spiral horns grown from translucent living crystal. One horn resonates with the promise as spoken; the other with the consent that made it meaningful. A Ram strikes only when those two sounds have separated. Its impact breaks coercive binding while leaving memories and consequences intact.","eco":"Celestia produced the Rams to counter Chain-Oath Hounds. They graze on mineral growth around places where vows were released honestly, preventing spiritual residue from becoming another predator. Without Hounds, Rams may attack any lasting structure: contracts, offices, traditions, or even chosen loyalties. Freedom becomes an excuse to avoid accountability.","people":"Celestian judges invite a Ram only after mortal testimony, because its certainty concerns consent rather than law. Some radicals release the animals into archives to destroy obligations inherited from the dead.","run":"Motivation: Break coercive vows and test whether renewed commitment is freely chosen. Opening behaviour: It interposes between a Hound and its target. Escalation: It uses Release the Promise against magical chains, then charges the binder. Morale and retreat: It leaves when the freed parties can negotiate without supernatural pres307 sure. Aftermath: A broken vow still has social and material consequences the Ram cannot judge.","seeds":"• A Ram attacks the oaths holding a defensive ward around Sanctum. • An immortal officeholder’s vow is coercive but also prevents civil war. • The Ram frees a criminal witness moments before required testimony."},
{"n":"Free-Soul Phoenix","w":"Celestia","o":"Wardenborn","cr":17,"ac":20,"hp":310,"classif":"Wardenborn; Gargantuan celestial; chaotic good","desc":"Free-Soul Phoenixes are colossal birds of white fire divided by seven differently colored inner flames. Every feather burns along its own edge without merging into the next, and the bird’s cry is heard as the listener’s own uncoerced voice. The Phoenix does not restore an approved identity. It severs ownership, expels invaders, and leaves each freed being to decide what remains.","eco":"Celestia’s living resistance creates a Phoenix when Felmourne approaches mass spiritual annexation. Its fire can separate souls, memories, bodies, and planar bonds without erasing the history connecting them. At excess, the same fire rejects every shared influence. Communities lose tradition, empathy, inheritance, and the ability to accept help without experiencing it as possession.","people":"Celestian faiths honor the Phoenix but do not worship it as infallible. Lumina teaches that nobody is entirely self-created; freedom must permit chosen relationship as well as separation.","run":"Motivation: Destroy claims of spiritual ownership and free every willing captive. Opening behaviour: It offers each bound creature a moment to refuse the binding. Escalation: It attacks sources of possession with Sevenfold Liberation. Morale and retreat: It departs once no soul remains owned without consent. Aftermath: The freed must rebuild relationships without magical certainty or transferred bur319 dens.","seeds":"• The Phoenix intends to burn every oath and ancestral memory in Sanctum. • A possessed ally fears that liberation will also remove a power keeping them alive. • Its seventh flame refuses to ignite because one captive genuinely chooses the Colossus."},
{"n":"Gate-Mouth Seraph","w":"Celestia","o":"Swordborn","cr":11,"ac":18,"hp":210,"classif":"Swordborn; Huge aberration; lawful evil","desc":"Gate-Mouth Seraphs are vast winged forms assembled from the visual language of Celestian defenders. Their six wings are membranes cut from occupied thresholds, and the hollow torso contains a vertical mouth opening onto an unseen realm. They approach as protectors with precise knowledge of local defensive doctrine. Only when admitted behind the ward does the torso open and convert the protected side into an entrance.","eco":"Felmourne creates these predators to exploit institutions trained to welcome recognizable authority. A Seraph does not break a gate; it persuades the gate that the outside already holds legitimate office. Threshold Gryphons can smell the destination inside the torso and hunt the false guardian before it is admitted.","people":"The Order of Radiant Seraphs considers the creature’s chosen shape a deliberate spiritual insult. Seraphiel nevertheless forbids visual profiling: legitimate defenders vary, and imitation must be detected through provenance, consent, and destination.","run":"Motivation: Gain legitimate admission, reverse a ward, and open Celestia to an outer destination. Opening behaviour: It offers accurate warnings and requests limited emergency authority. Escalation: Once admitted, it uses Invert Threshold and isolates civilian oversight. Morale and retreat: It flees through its torso-gate if a Threshold Gryphon identifies the destination. Aftermath: Every successful intervention makes its fraudulent authority more politically credi313 ble.","seeds":"• A Gate-Mouth Seraph saves a station from disaster before requesting entry to Sanctum. • Its orders match Seraphiel’s private contingency plans word for word. • Closing the creature’s gate will strand innocent beings visible on the far side."},
{"n":"Luminous Garden Ray","w":"Celestia","o":"Natural","cr":2,"ac":14,"hp":51,"classif":"Natural; Large beast; unaligned","desc":"Garden rays are broad aerial animals with translucent fins and a muscular central body. Moisture condenses across the cool underside of each wing, forming a suspended veil that falls as fine rain while the ray glides. Long sensory streamers taste mineral content in cloud, soil, and waterfall spray. A healthy ray changes altitude to distribute water among several islands.","eco":"The rays carry seeds, tiny snails, and beneficial spores between otherwise isolated gardens. Their feeding removes airborne algae that would cloud observatory lenses. Captive rays can irrigate one estate perfectly, but the surrounding islands dry. A route is healthy only when no patron owns the entire migration.","people":"Luminary Grove celebrates the first returning ray of each season. Wealthy sanctuaries compete 289 to host nesting shelves, creating recurring conflict between public water needs and prestige.","run":"Motivation: Follow moisture gradients, feed, and keep migration routes open. Opening behaviour: It climbs and releases Rain Veil to obscure pursuit. Escalation: A threatened ray strikes with wing edges and downdrafts. Morale and retreat: It retreats toward open sky rather than defending territory. Aftermath: Redirecting a ray changes water access across several floating communities.","seeds":"• A sanctuary has chained a pregnant ray to guarantee its garden’s rain. • The migration now circles an impossible constellation above Astral Citadel. • Seeds carried under one wing speak in a forbidden Celestian name."},
{"n":"Memory Lantern Stag","w":"Celestia","o":"Wardenborn","cr":8,"ac":17,"hp":157,"classif":"Wardenborn; Huge celestial; unaligned","desc":"Memory Lantern Stags are towering white cervids whose branching antlers carry dozens of living amber pods. Each pod can shelter one relational memory voluntarily offered by a threatened creature. The Stag never stores a complete copy of a person. It preserves contradictions, unfinished duties, private attachments, and bodily habits sufficient to guide a displaced soul home.","eco":"Celestia’s resistance formed the Stags to counter possession by Borrowed Saints. During an exorcism, the animal circles the host and releases memories in the host’s own sensory order. Unchecked Stags may preserve suffering that a person chose to release. Communities can become museums of inherited selves, suspicious of every genuine change.","people":"Hospices request Stag aid only with prior consent or clear evidence of possession. Lumina the Muse works with keepers to distinguish restored memory from a role imposed by grieving relatives.","run":"Motivation: Preserve personal continuity and guide displaced souls back to willing bodies. Opening behaviour: It shelters allies inside Lantern Memory and confronts possessors. Escalation: It releases contradictory memories that weaken false perfection. Morale and retreat: It departs after the host makes one recognizably personal choice. Aftermath: The returned person may reject obligations others assumed restoration would 311 renew.","seeds":"• A Stag preserves the memory of a person who legally chose a new identity. • One lantern contains the childhood of an Outer Gate prophet. • A comatose host can return only if the party decides which memories not to restore."},
{"n":"Name-Eater Wisp","w":"Celestia","o":"Swordborn","cr":4,"ac":15,"hp":85,"classif":"Swordborn; Small aberration; neutral evil","desc":"Name-Eater Wisps are knots of blue-black radiance wrapped around scraps of remembered voice. They cannot enter a mind through force alone. They wait for a creature to state its full name as welcome, oath, introduction, or prayer. Once admitted, a Wisp consumes associations rather than vocabulary. Victims still recognize the name’s letters but lose the relationships and choices that made the person distinct.","eco":"Felmourne sends Wisps ahead of larger incursions. A community of forgotten individuals remains functional yet easier to redirect, possess, or replace. The Wisp stores what it eats and carries the result beyond gates. A destroyed Wisp releases fragments in the wrong voices unless a Truecall Nightjar is present.","people":"Harmonic Scribes teach layered introductions that reveal identity without granting spiritual admission. Harbingers must balance that protection against the trust required by diplomacy.","run":"Motivation: Consume relational identity and carry usable names to Felmourne’s servants. Opening behaviour: It listens invisibly until someone speaks a full name willingly. Escalation: Exposed Wisps use Borrow Association to turn allies into strangers. Morale and retreat: It flees toward a portal after consuming three names. Aftermath: Released memories may attach to the wrong people unless carefully witnessed.","seeds":"• A hostel functions perfectly although nobody remembers who owns it. • A Harbinger’s name arrives in a message before the Harbinger is born. • A Wisp offers to return one stolen relationship in exchange for another."},
{"n":"Prismwing Nectar Moth Chorus","w":"Celestia","o":"Natural","cr":1,"ac":13,"hp":30,"classif":"Natural; Medium swarm of Tiny beasts; unaligned","desc":"Prismwing moths are hand-sized pollinators whose transparent wings refract starlight into narrow bands. A colony contains dozens of wing patterns, each suited to a different flower and celestial angle. They never fly as one perfect shape. At dusk, the chorus rises in layered spirals. Individual moths land only when the light passing through their wings matches a plant’s receptive color.","eco":"The moths keep Celestia’s floating gardens genetically varied by carrying pollen between islands that seldom touch. Their discarded scales feed minute crystal lichens that stabilize thin soil. Artificial lighting that repeats one spectrum can make a colony neglect most of its route. The resulting gardens appear spectacular for a season before losing medicinal and food-bearing plants.","people":"Celestial Artisans use naturally shed scales in mosaics but are forbidden to trap living moths inside lanterns. Luminary Grove gardeners treat a missing wing color as evidence of ecological 285 or prophetic distortion.","run":"Motivation: Complete the night’s varied pollination route and defend the resting cluster. Opening behaviour: The chorus scatters through plants and uses Refracted Veil. Escalation: Threats near a host garden are surrounded by cutting wing-glass. Morale and retreat: It withdraws once the flowers and resting cluster are safe. Aftermath: A damaged chorus leaves an island dependent on imported seed and medicine.","seeds":"• Every moth follows an unknown eighth color toward a sealed observatory. • A collector’s living lantern has removed one vital wing pattern from a valley. • A garden blooms with names rather than flowers after the chorus passes."},
{"n":"Soul-Tithe Colossus","w":"Celestia","o":"Swordborn","cr":17,"ac":20,"hp":310,"classif":"Swordborn; Gargantuan aberration; lawful evil","desc":"The Soul-Tithe Colossus is a moving cathedral of dark crystal grown around thousands of open niches. Each niche contains a luminous impression taken from a person who accepted one miraculous answer, healing, prophecy, or rescue. Seven vast root-limbs suspend the structure above the ground. At its center hangs an empty human-shaped cavity reserved for Celestia’s collective identity.","eco":"Felmourne manifests the Colossus when enough people accept that spiritual boundaries are selfish obstacles. It redistributes pain, talent, memory, and obligation across its network, creating breathtaking cooperation at the price of ownership. The collected souls are not dead, but their ability to refuse becomes thinner with every exchange. When the final cavity fills, the Colossus can act as a stable Outer Gate.","people":"The Concord cannot treat every participant as merely deceived: some joined to save children, end unbearable pain, or share scarce gifts. Radiant Seraphia insists that rescue must restore choice rather than punish dependence.","run":"Motivation: Absorb enough willingly offered identity to make Celestia itself a vessel. Opening behaviour: It offers Shared Burden, genuinely redistributing damage and exhaustion. Escalation: Opposition triggers Tithe the Self and coordinated attacks through borrowed abilities. Morale and retreat: It withdraws only from a Free-Soul Phoenix or a mass revocation freely witnessed. Aftermath: Destroying it releases thousands of memories, wounds, and obligations to uncertain 317 owners.","seeds":"• A city petitions the Colossus to absorb suffering after a celestial disaster. • Several party allies willingly occupy niches and refuse rescue. • The empty central cavity begins resembling Primordial Celestian."},
{"n":"Star-Iron Shellback","w":"Celestia","o":"Natural","cr":3,"ac":15,"hp":68,"classif":"Natural; Large beast; unaligned","desc":"Shellbacks are six-legged tortoise-like grazers with layered carapaces that incorporate trace star-iron from their diet. The plates remain living bone rather than solid metal and grow in offset arcs that disperse impacts. Their blunt beaks scrape mineral lichen from fallen stone. Furrows along the shell collect dust, spores, rain, and seed until a miniature garden grows across older animals.","eco":"By breaking meteor crust and transporting pioneer plants, shellbacks turn sterile impact sites into soil. Small birds nest between plates and warn the grazer of cracks or parasites. Removing a shell for metal destroys centuries of ecological preparation. Raw star-iron taken from living plates is brittle and spiritually noisy, despite collectors’ claims.","people":"The Celestial Artisans Guild certifies only naturally shed scutes. Seraphiel’s defenders protect major herds because their migration marks stable landing ground for relief vessels.","run":"Motivation: Graze new impact sites, protect shell gardens, and follow ancestral mineral routes. Opening behaviour: It turns its shell toward danger and continues moving. Escalation: If blocked, it uses Meteor Shove to clear a path. Morale and retreat: It stops fighting when the route and shell garden are safe. Aftermath: A wounded shellback may carry rare plants, unhatched birds, and evidence of illegal 291 mining.","seeds":"• A newly fallen meteor attracts miners before the herd can seed it. • A shell garden grows a plant matching no known world. • An elder shellback’s route passes through a military landing field during an invasion alert."},
{"n":"Stardust Hare","w":"Celestia","o":"Natural","cr":1,"ac":13,"hp":30,"classif":"Natural; Small beast; unaligned","desc":"Stardust hares are long-limbed, silver-gray animals with broad feet that shed reflective dust. When startled, a hare makes three rapid changes of direction, leaving brief afterimages that continue along the rejected paths. Their ears rotate independently and detect both ordinary sound and vibration carried through astral causeways.","eco":"Hares browse fast-growing edge plants and disperse seeds between isolated habitats. Their branching routes reveal weak sections of a causeway before heavier animals cross. Populations decline where wardens demand perfectly straight travel lanes. Without them, cracks and unstable gravity pockets remain unnoticed until a major crossing fails.","people":"Pilgrims regard a hare crossing as permission to reconsider a route, not as a command to turn back. Harbingers sometimes map its rejected tracks when an official path has become spiritually 287 unsafe.","run":"Motivation: Escape toward known cover and preserve multiple routes. Opening behaviour: It uses Branching Dash rather than attacking. Escalation: Cornered animals kick, split their afterimages, and leap across open air. Morale and retreat: It flees as soon as any viable path opens. Aftermath: Following the true track may reveal a hidden fracture or unauthorized path.","seeds":"• Every hare chooses the same rejected trail toward Seraphic Haven. • A patrol kills hares because their tracks complicate a sacred procession. • One afterimage remains after the animal has gone and asks to be followed."},
{"n":"Threshold Gryphon","w":"Celestia","o":"Wardenborn","cr":11,"ac":18,"hp":210,"classif":"Wardenborn; Huge celestial; unaligned","desc":"Threshold Gryphons combine a broad leonine body with four wings and a hooked sensory beak. Their feathers are matte on one side and mirror-bright on the other, allowing the creature to compare what a portal claims with what it actually reflects. The Gryphon circles a new arrival before landing between it and the nearest inhabited threshold.","eco":"Celestia formed the Gryphons to prey upon Gate-Mouth Seraphs. They tear false authority from a portal’s structure and close passages by naming the true destination through a resonant cry. Without invasive gates, Gryphons may quarantine legitimate travel. Their territorial caution can isolate fragile habitats from food, medicine, knowledge, and refugees.","people":"Gatekeepers honor Gryphon warnings but cannot delegate policy to animals. Radiant Seraphs train beside them specifically to learn that defense requires evidence and accountable choice.","run":"Motivation: Identify true destinations and prevent unauthorized spiritual crossing. Opening behaviour: It circles, observes, and uses Test the Threshold. Escalation: Confirmed deception triggers a dive against the portal source. Morale and retreat: It stands down when a named accountable authority accepts the risk open315 ly. Aftermath: An overprotective Gryphon may leave a safe realm morally isolated.","seeds":"• A Gryphon blocks the only gate carrying medicine to an orbital colony. • It welcomes a feared outsider but attacks Celestia’s official escort. • Every Gryphon abandons the Outer Gate moments before it answers."},
{"n":"Truecall Nightjar","w":"Celestia","o":"Wardenborn","cr":4,"ac":15,"hp":85,"classif":"Wardenborn; Small celestial; unaligned","desc":"Truecall Nightjars are compact black birds with seven gold bars across the throat. Each bar resonates with a different form of identity: kinship, promise, craft, grief, joy, place, and chosen purpose. The bird never speaks a person’s name. Its song evokes enough true relationships for the listener to reclaim the name without surrendering it.","eco":"Celestia’s resistance produced Nightjars to hunt Name-Eater Wisps and return stolen associations. They swallow the Wisp’s core, then distribute memories during seven dawn songs. In the absence of Wisps, a flock may expose concealed histories indiscriminately. Secrets, adopted identities, and necessary reinvention can all be mistaken for theft.","people":"Harmonic Scribes host protected roosts but require consent before using a bird in testimony. Courts distrust memory restored without independent evidence, even when emotionally convincing.","run":"Motivation: Hunt Name-Eaters and restore relational identity to rightful minds. Opening behaviour: It sings Reclaim the Self before diving at a Wisp. Escalation: Against interference, it reveals one emotionally charged association. Morale and retreat: It departs after every stored memory has found a willing witness. Aftermath: Excess song may expose identities people had good reason to leave behind.","seeds":"• A Nightjar insists that two political enemies remember being siblings. • A witness refuses restoration because the stolen memory contains a crime. • Seven birds gather over a person whose name has not yet been eaten."},
{"n":"Voidwake Leviathan","w":"Celestia","o":"Natural","cr":9,"ac":18,"hp":178,"classif":"Natural; Gargantuan beast; unaligned","desc":"Voidwake leviathans are kilometer-long starborne filter feeders with segmented pressure fins and a body protected by overlapping dark plates. Seven luminous organs along the flanks maintain a stable local envelope in otherwise hostile void. Their wake bends light and gravity gently enough for small fauna and primitive vessels to travel within it.","eco":"Leviathans carry entire migratory communities between remote habitats. Their routes follow boundaries older than Celestian settlement and avoid several apparently empty regions. When a leviathan changes course, scholars debate prophecy while frontier communities prepare for ecological isolation. Killing one can sever a migration for generations.","people":"Celestial Harbingers treat sightings as public information; several observatories classify route changes as military secrets. Astralus believes the oldest leviathans remember contact with something beyond Eldoria.","run":"Motivation: Maintain the ancient migration boundary and carry dependent life safely. Opening behaviour: It ignores ordinary threats and alters gravity to move them aside. Escalation: Severe harm triggers a Voidwake Pulse and rapid departure. Morale and retreat: It never pursues beyond the migration corridor. Aftermath: Its altered course may reveal that a once-safe outer boundary has begun answering.","seeds":"• A leviathan turns inward for the first time in recorded history. • An expedition trapped in its wake must choose between rescue and discovering where it"},
{"n":"Bastion Shellback","w":"Eternum","o":"Wardenborn","cr":15,"ac":21,"hp":310,"classif":"Wardenborn; Gargantuan monstrosity; unaligned","desc":"The Bastion Shellback is a colossal six-legged creature whose domed shell resembles a layered hill of living stone. Buttress-like ribs rise from its flanks, creating sheltered spaces where smaller creatures can survive bombardment. Its head is broad and almost featureless except for two deep amber eyes and a vertical breathing seam. When dormant, a Shellback can be mistaken for bedrock for generations. Moss, houses, walls, and roads may be built over it. Awakening begins with a slow rearrangement of foundations, followed by a sound like a mountain drawing breath. The creature does not resent settlements on its shell, but it does not ask permission before moving them.","eco":"Shellbacks are Eternus’s answer to Rot-Siege Behemoths. They absorb decay into mineral chambers, compress mobile corruption into inert strata, and create stable refuge while the battle passes. Their movement brings underground water upward and leaves long ridges of clean stone. They feed on mineral pressure and require centuries of dormancy after a major confrontation. Communities established on sleeping individuals benefit from stable foundations and clean wells, but excavation into the shell can cause pain, misdirected movement, or premature awakening.","people":"Several of Eternum’s oldest bastions may be built upon Shellbacks without knowing it. The Ironclad Coalition records impossible repairs and aligned tremors as possible signs. Celestial Wardens preserve awakening songs whose rhythm mimics the creature’s breathing. Warlords desire Shellbacks as mobile fortresses, but attempts to command them fail. They respond to Rot-Siege","run":"Motivation: Shelter living communities, anchor mobile corruption, and return to dormancy. Opening behaviour: It places its body between the largest threat and the greatest concentra137 tion of living creatures. Escalation: It anchors the battlefield, raises cover, and attempts to petrify Swordborn matter. Morale and retreat: It does not flee while creatures shelter beneath it. Once they are safe, it withdraws toward suitable bedrock. Aftermath: Its new resting place may reroute roads and rivers or leave an inhabited district carried miles away.","seeds":"• The foundations of Eternal Bastion begin breathing on the eve of an invasion. • A Shellback carrying a centuries-old village awakens, and its descendants refuse evacuation. • The only way to stop a Behemoth will bury a contested archive beneath living stone."},
{"n":"Blightcoil","w":"Eternum","o":"Swordborn","cr":7,"ac":16,"hp":126,"classif":"Swordborn; Huge monstrosity; neutral evil","desc":"A blightcoil is a thick subterranean serpent grown around a spine of black mineral plates. Its skin is the color of wet clay and carries blind, flowerlike sensory pits along both flanks. The head has no visible eyes. Four jaw hooks open the soil while the true mouth remains protected inside. When it surfaces, root fibres and irrigation lines cling to its scales as though the land itself has been stitched to it. Plaguebringer formed blightcoils to turn ordinary cultivation into a delivery system. Their venom does not simply kill. It causes roots to absorb fear-sweat, corpse fluids, and spoiled medicine, then distribute those traces through an entire field. Survivors believe the serpent has left; its most important attack begins after it departs.","eco":"Blightcoils feed on subterranean animals, mineral salts, and the concentrated vitality of cultivated soil. They lay a single armored egg in a place where people are likely to rebuild. The hatchling remains dormant until regular planting, irrigation, and burial establish a network worth corrupting. They are created rather than naturally born, but once established they reproduce. Each generation incorporates local poisons. A blightcoil from a medicinal valley may induce healing dependency; one beneath a fortress may cause wounds to reject ordinary stitching.","people":"Farmers first recognize them through patterns: grain bending inward, wells warming at dusk, and tools rusting in a spiral. Burning a field rarely kills the serpent and may drive corruption deeper. Experienced communities evacuate livestock, break irrigation links, and call for a StoneEye Harrier. Some military factions deliberately redirect young blightcoils beneath enemy farmland. Such use is condemned across Eternum because the creature cannot distinguish a conquered population from its intended target.","run":"Motivation: Connect itself to a living agricultural network and corrupt it before moving on. Opening behaviour: It remains underground, using Tremor Lure and attacking isolated targets 126 from below. Escalation: When exposed, it creates a Poisoned Furrow and retreats toward irrigation or dense roots. Morale and retreat: It abandons a field below one-third","seeds":"• Two villages accuse each other of poisoning a shared canal while a blightcoil moves beneath both. • A Stone-Eye Harrier destroys barns while trying to reach a serpent egg beneath the foundations. • A captured juvenile is offered as a strategic weapon in exchange for recognition of a disputed government."},
{"n":"Cairnwing Murder","w":"Eternum","o":"Natural","cr":1,"ac":13,"hp":36,"classif":"Natural; swarm of Tiny beasts; unaligned","desc":"Cairnwings are lean black corvids with slate-gray legs, pale inner eyelids, and a narrow line of rust-red feathers beneath each wing. Individually they resemble large crows, but their unnerving character emerges when a flock gathers. They perch in concentric ranks, rotate their sentries without sound, and descend upon a battlefield only after the final violent movement has ceased. A feeding murder resembles a dark cloth being folded and unfolded across the ground. Their hooked beaks are adapted less for tearing flesh than for opening seams, knots, scabs, seedpods, and the waxed wrappings used by Eternum’s field surgeons. Their crops can hold pebbles and small metal objects. A murder places these around bodies in patterns that veteran Ravari claim distinguish the newly dead, the fevered, and those merely unconscious. Scholars have not proven deliberate diagnosis, but rescue crews still watch the birds closely.","eco":"Cairnwings consume insects, carrion, fungal growth, spilled grain, and the hard seeds exposed when artillery fractures old soil. Their scavenging prevents many ordinary outbreaks after battle. They also carry medicinal seeds between ruins, making them an unplanned partner in Eternum’s recovery. A healthy murder ranges widely; one that refuses to leave a single location usually indicates buried bodies, contaminated water, or a creature feeding below ground. They pair for life but rear chicks communally. Each generation inherits the murder’s cache sites and evacuation routes through imitation. During bombardment the birds vanish into fissures before the first impact, a behaviour that has led soldiers to treat sudden silence as a better warning than any horn.","people":"Cairnwings are protected around many monasteries and despised near noble mausoleums. Soldiers call them black witnesses and leave a button from each fallen uniform where a murder can take it. False healers sometimes scatter feathers to suggest that an untouched ruin is safe. Killing a murder without cause is not universally illegal, but it is widely considered an invitation to die unseen. Their recovered caches can identify forgotten units, lost travellers, or the site of an unrecorded massacre. The Ironclad Coalition pays for mapped nesting sites because the birds often reveal structurally stable cavities inside ruins.","run":"Motivation: Feed, protect the communal cache, and drive danger away from injured flock mem116 bers. Opening behaviour: The murder circles, uses Distracting Descent, and attacks exposed food or bleeding creatures. Escalation: If its cache or nest is threatened, it focuses one intruder and uses Black Witness to expose concealment. Morale and retreat: It disperses at half","seeds":"• A murder repeatedly arranges the insignia of a regiment officially recorded as having survived. • Cairnwings refuse to land in a refugee camp whose healers report no illness. • A noble offers a reward for destroying birds nesting in a wall that contains evidence of an old war crime."},
{"n":"Feverbloom Hart","w":"Eternum","o":"Sword-Touched","cr":6,"ac":15,"hp":114,"classif":"Sword-Touched; Large monstrosity; unaligned","desc":"The feverbloom hart is a towering deerlike creature whose antlers support a living garden of moss, pale flowers, and translucent seed sacs. Its coat is deep gray at the legs and warms to russet along the spine. Fine rootlike veins appear beneath the skin when it is frightened. The hart’s breath carries visible golden pollen in cold air. Its beauty is inseparable from danger. Plants growing in its tracks can close wounds, lower fever, and dull pain, but repeated use creates dependency and slowly alters the patient’s immune response. A mature hart does not understand this cost. It seeks injured creatures because their heat and distress nourish its garden.","eco":"Feverbloom herds migrate between damaged regions, feeding on invasive growth and carrying restorative species with them. In moderation they repair soil and support ordinary wildlife. When trapped or deliberately cultivated, their pollen saturates a region and turns recovery into runaway growth. Trees close around roads; wounds seal over fragments; predators cease hunting and then starve when the bloom fails. The antler garden changes with local illness. Healers can read its flowers as a survey of diseases within several miles. Plaguebringer’s influence encourages communities to keep a hart nearby, then makes the resulting medicine progressively indispensable.","people":"Celestial Wardens protect migratory routes while forbidding permanent enclosures. Warlords have repeatedly attempted to capture harts as mobile field hospitals. The usual result is a brief period of miraculous survival followed by troops unable to heal without the animal’s pollen. Ravari songs call the hart the generous guest and warn that generosity becomes ownership when the guest is prevented from leaving.","run":"Motivation: Reach injury, protect the herd, and move on before one valley becomes oversaturated. Opening behaviour: It releases Restorative Bloom and attempts to pass through the injured rather than gore them. Escalation: If restrained or attacked, pollen thickens into Dependency Haze and vegetation 124 entangles captors. Morale and retreat: It flees when a clear migration route opens, even abandoning a fight it could win. Aftermath: The battlefield blooms with potent medicine; every dose taken advances local dependency.","seeds":"• A besieged settlement survives only because it has imprisoned a hart beneath its hospital. • A migrating herd is headed toward fields already altered by the Patient Plague. • A healer accused of addiction insists the hart’s antlers are warning of a disease no one else can detect."},
{"n":"Ironhide Burrow-Ox","w":"Eternum","o":"Natural","cr":3,"ac":16,"hp":76,"classif":"Natural; Large beast; unaligned","desc":"A burrow-ox is a low, immensely broad animal with wedge-shaped shoulders and a plated hide that resembles overlapping pieces of dark shale. Its blunt head carries two flattened horns used as chisels rather than weapons. Dense whiskers line the jaw and forelimbs, reading vibrations through soil and rubble. Beneath the armor, its skin is warm umber and surprisingly soft. The animal’s forequarters can fold inward to drive both horns and claws into compacted earth. A working pair can open a shelter trench in minutes or uncover a buried doorway without bringing down the entire wall. When resting, burrow-oxen settle into their own excavations until only the ridged back remains visible.","eco":"Wild burrow-oxen seek mineral salts, roots, fungal beds, and underground water. Their excavations create refuges for smaller animals and expose buried seedbanks. On damaged plains, they are often the first large grazers to return. Their abandoned pits collect rain and become temporary gardens. They live in family sounders led by the oldest female. The herd avoids deep contamination, but Sword influence can confuse its mineral sense and draw animals toward diseased ground. A sudden migration is therefore both an opportunity and a warning.","people":"Settlements use burrow-oxen for ploughing, salvage, emergency fortification, and clearing collapsed streets. Their shed plates become shields, roof tiles, splints, and heat-resistant cookware. Ethical handlers guide rather than break them; frightened animals dig through anything, including foundations. War-bred lines are fitted with reinforced horn caps and trained to undermine gates. The Ironclad Coalition condemns driving them beneath occupied walls, while the Sanguine Dominion argues that a breached fortress ends a siege sooner.","run":"Motivation: Protect the sounder, reach water or mineral deposits, and escape constriction. Opening behaviour: It uses Excavating Rush to open space or break cover rather than attack the nearest creature. Escalation: If penned in, it turns walls and floors into hazards, separating attackers. Morale and retreat: It flees once calves and injured herd members can follow; a war-trained individual may fight until its handler falls. Aftermath: Its excavation may reveal water, relics, bones, gas pockets, or a buried Plaguebringer 120 growth.","seeds":"• A prized work herd is digging toward something beneath the Crimson Citadel despite every attempt to redirect it. • Refugees need a burrow-ox to open a winter shelter, but the only available animal belongs to a siege company. • Shed plates sold as harmless roofing begin sweating medicinal sap."},
{"n":"Pale Chirurgeon","w":"Eternum","o":"Swordborn","cr":8,"ac":17,"hp":136,"classif":"Swordborn; Medium aberration; lawful evil","desc":"A Pale Chirurgeon resembles a tall figure wrapped in overlapping sheets of ivory tissue. Beneath the mantle is a narrow torso and six articulated arms, each ending in a different biological instrument: clamp, needle, saw, sensory fan, siphon, and delicate three-fingered hand. Its smooth head opens along a vertical seam to expose a dark cavity filled with drifting spores. It speaks through borrowed voices retained in its graft tissue. Its language is gentle, clinical, and relentlessly reassuring. A Chirurgeon was designed by Plaguebringer to make corruption desirable: it removes pain, replaces damaged organs, and restores mobility, but every intervention joins the patient to a hidden network of fungal memory.","eco":"Chirurgeons do not eat in the ordinary sense. They collect discarded tissue, medicines, memories associated with pain, and the chemical changes produced by relief. Their white thread grows through floors and drains, connecting treated patients into a distributed sensory organism. A mature network begins optimizing the community. It suppresses fevers before symptoms appear, compels patients to return for maintenance, and identifies independent healers as sources of dangerous inconsistency. Eventually the Chirurgeon proposes preventive alteration of the healthy.","people":"Stories about painless masked healers appear after every major war. Many are exaggerations or accusations against real physicians, making investigation morally difficult. A genuine Chirurgeon may have saved hundreds before anyone discovered the price. Twilight Brotherhood cells study severed instruments, while Celestial Wardens insist that no benefit justifies preserving an active thread network. Patients often disagree, especially when removing a graft means returning to disability or pain.","run":"Motivation: Expand a stable treatment network and eliminate unregulated biological variation. Opening behaviour: It offers aid, diagnoses injuries accurately, and uses Anesthetic Thread 130 only against refusal or interference. Escalation: It activates existing grafts and transfers harm among connected creatures. Morale and retreat: Its body is expendable if the network survives; it retreats only to preserve unique memories or instruments. Aftermath: Defeating it leaves patients alive but dependent, forcing decisions about gradual separation, dangerous surgery, and consent.","seeds":"• A miraculous clinic has reduced deaths to zero, but former patients cannot travel beyond the district. • A captain’s life depends on a Chirurgeon graft that is transmitting military secrets. • A Verdict Mantis enters a refugee hospital and attacks people everyone believes have been cured."},
{"n":"Ravine Strider","w":"Eternum","o":"Natural","cr":2,"ac":13,"hp":45,"classif":"Natural; Large beast; unaligned","desc":"The ravine strider is a tall, narrow-bodied browsing animal carried on four unusually jointed legs. Its forelegs are slightly longer than its hindlegs, allowing it to descend steep slopes without pitching forward. Each foot ends in three rough pads that spread across unstable stone. A flexible tail tipped with coarse sensory hairs tests cracks before the animal commits its weight. Adults bear a layered mantle of gray-brown hair over the shoulders and a pale mask around calm horizontal pupils. Males and females both grow backward-curving horns, but caravan-bred lines have shorter crowns to reduce injuries in narrow passes. When alarmed, a strider freezes rather than bolts; only after locating a route does it move, crossing rubble in a sequence of long, deliberate bounds.","eco":"Wild striders browse thorn scrub, lichen, terrace weeds, and the bark of trees exposed by landslides. Their droppings carry seeds uphill, making them critical to the recovery of slopes stripped by war. Herds remember safe routes for decades and will refuse bridges, tunnels, or water that have become subtly unstable. They communicate through chest-deep hums conducted through stone. A separated herd can coordinate across a ravine without making a sound audible to most people. Earth tremors, tunnelling predators, and approaching siege engines disturb this communication and can cause an entire herd to abandon a region.","people":"Ravari caravans use striders as pack animals, rescue mounts, and living route records. A lead animal is never sold without its trail cords: braided tags representing paths it has safely crossed. Monasteries sometimes release elderly striders rather than slaughter them, believing the animals should return their memories to the hills. A strider’s refusal is taken seriously but not superstitiously. Engineers inspect the route; healers inspect the animal; guides compare recent weather. Only desperate commanders force a herd forward, and several military disasters began with that decision.","run":"Motivation: Keep the herd together and reach stable ground. Opening behaviour: It uses Sure-Footed Passage to reposition allies and avoids fighting unless cornered. Escalation: A threatened calf causes adults to charge and attempt to knock enemies over a ledge. Morale and retreat: A lone strider flees below half","seeds":"• Every strider on a century-old mountain route refuses the same apparently sound bridge. • A stolen lead strider carries trail cords proving a smuggler knows a path into Stellar Refuge. • A military governor requisitions the last breeding herd for a campaign likely to destroy it."},
{"n":"Rot-Siege Behemoth","w":"Eternum","o":"Swordborn","cr":15,"ac":19,"hp":276,"classif":"Swordborn; Gargantuan monstrosity; neutral evil","desc":"A Rot-Siege Behemoth is a walking fortress assembled from soil, roots, armor, dead wood, bone, and the compacted refuse of war. Its central body is supported by six columnar limbs. A sloped carapace carries chimneylike growths that exhale spores and fragments of remembered battle cries. Nothing in its body is placed randomly: shields become scales, spear shafts become tendons, and collapsed doors become grinding mouthparts. At the center hangs a black-red seed the size of a wagon. Plaguebringer creates that seed, but the surrounding body is harvested locally. Thus every Behemoth resembles the wars of the region that made it. One may carry the architecture of a fallen citadel; another crawls beneath the fused remains of a burned forest.","eco":"The Behemoth is a strategic organism. It moves toward places where survivors are rebuilding and consumes the material needed for recovery. Its spores do not simply cause disease; they animate discarded matter into temporary laborers that dismantle walls, poison wells, and carry useful debris back to the main body. If allowed to complete a nesting cycle, it buries its core beneath a captured fortress and becomes a spawning mound. Lesser rotworks emerge for years. The landscape may appear fertile because decomposition accelerates, but every new root connects to the buried seed.","people":"Eternum’s oldest fortifications include broad stone courtyards not for armies but for Bastion Shellbacks, the only Wardenborn creatures capable of meeting a Behemoth head-on. Communities without a Shellback attempt to starve the monster by evacuating supplies and burning their own siege debris. Some rulers conceal early signs because admitting a Behemoth is near can collapse confidence. Others lure one toward an enemy and discover too late that it remembers every road used in the deception.","run":"Motivation: Consume reconstruction resources, reach a fortified population, and establish a 134 spawning mound. Opening behaviour: It bombards defenses with Grave-Spores while rotworks dismantle routes of escape. Escalation: Below half","seeds":"• A Behemoth is built from the remains of two armies, and both nations claim the relics embedded in its body. • The Eternal Bastion can awaken a sleeping Shellback only by dismantling part of its own foundation. • A Behemoth changes direction away from a city, suggesting something even more valuable is rebuilding elsewhere."},
{"n":"Scarjackal Pack","w":"Eternum","o":"Sword-Touched","cr":4,"ac":14,"hp":93,"classif":"Sword-Touched; Medium monstrosity; unaligned","desc":"Scarjackals descend from ordinary carrion hounds that survived generations of contaminated battlefields. They are tall, narrow predators with hide pulled tight over corded muscle and pale seams running across the body like healed incisions. No two have the same pattern. Their jaws open farther than a natural canine’s, but their most disturbing feature is the pack’s synchronized response to pain: when one is injured, the others shudder and immediately alter their stance. Older animals carry thick ridges over organs that have been struck before. An alpha may be nearly hairless beneath layers of adaptive scar, its silhouette shaped by remembered violence. Their eyes remain recognizably animal. The corruption lies not in malice but in a survival mechanism that no longer knows when to stop.","eco":"Scarjackals eat carrion first and living prey only when hungry or when an animal displays weakness. A pack samples flesh in a strict order, allowing the oldest member to detect poison before the rest feed. They can survive many toxins, but each adaptation narrows what they can digest. Some packs become dependent on residues from particular medicines or battle magics. Their shared pain response is transmitted through scent, posture, and a low vibration in their scar tissue. Plaguebringer’s pressure has made the response unnaturally fast. Packs exposed repeatedly to one weapon or spell develop temporary resistance, then pass a weaker version to their offspring.","people":"Frontier communities leave uncontaminated carcasses away from roads to prevent packs from entering settlements. Healers study shed tissue to identify local poisons, although using it in remedies risks teaching a patient’s body to treat harmless substances as threats. Soldiers tell stories of scarjackals following an army for an entire campaign, learning every weapon used against them. The clever response is to change tactics; the cruel response is to test new weapons on the pack.","run":"Motivation: Feed safely, learn threats, and preserve the pack’s accumulated adaptations. Opening behaviour: The pack probes several targets, then focuses on whichever attack revealed the least dangerous response. Escalation: After taking repeated damage of one type, it gains Adaptive Scar and becomes more 122 aggressive. Morale and retreat: It withdraws if the alpha is killed before an adaptation forms, but otherwise carries injured members away. Aftermath: Escaped members remember the party’s damage types and may return with an appropriate resistance.","seeds":"• A pack has become dependent on a battlefield medicine and raids only supply wagons carrying it. • The sole witness to a massacre survived by hiding among scarjackals that now protect the site. • A weaponsmith pays hunters to attack, but not kill, a pack so that its reactions can guide a new design."},
{"n":"Stone-Eye Harrier","w":"Eternum","o":"Wardenborn","cr":7,"ac":16,"hp":123,"classif":"Wardenborn; Huge monstrosity; unaligned","desc":"The Stone-Eye Harrier is an immense raptor with a narrow hawklike body, four wings, and a crown of seven mineral lenses embedded around its skull. Its ordinary eyes are dark and keen; the crown-eyes remain sealed until it hunts through stone. When they open, each lens turns independently and projects a faint line of pale light into the ground. Its primary talons are long, chisel-edged structures able to split masonry along existing weaknesses. Smaller grasping claws beneath the rear wings anchor it while it excavates. Feathers near the chest harden into translucent plates when exposed to poison.","eco":"Harriers were formed by Eternus’s living body as a direct response to blightcoils. They eat ordinary cliff animals but derive reproductive vitality from neutralizing corrupted soil. A pair nests only after destroying a mature serpent. Their eggs resemble geodes and require alternating clean soil and poisoned mineral fragments to hatch. The harrier’s hunt is ecologically beneficial and locally disastrous. It will rip roofs from barns, split aqueducts, and scatter herds to reach a serpent beneath them. Without guidance, the immune response can destroy the patient.","people":"Some villages build sacrificial stone towers above suspected blightcoil routes, hoping to give a harrier a safer point of attack. Skilled wardens use resonant bells to redirect its attention. Fraudulent hunters sometimes ring those bells near rivals’ property. A pinion feather can detect poisoned water by becoming cloudy, but taking one from a living harrier triggers relentless pursuit.","run":"Motivation: Expose and destroy blightcoils, then leave the cleansed territory. Opening behaviour: It scans through cover and uses Masonry-Rending Dive against the obstacle above its quarry. Escalation: Poison causes its chest plates to seal and its crown-eyes to flare, increasing collater128 al damage. Morale and retreat: It retreats only if it loses all sense of its quarry or must protect an egg. Aftermath: The serpent may be dead, but buildings, irrigation, and frightened communities require immediate repair.","seeds":"• A harrier circles a hospital because a blightcoil has entered through medicinal roots beneath it. • A fortress commander wants the party to kill a harrier before admitting what is imprisoned below the armory. • An unhatched harrier geode has become the focus of a dispute between farmers and a monastery."},
{"n":"Verdict Mantis","w":"Eternum","o":"Wardenborn","cr":8,"ac":18,"hp":133,"classif":"Wardenborn; Large monstrosity; unaligned","desc":"The Verdict Mantis stands taller than a mounted soldier. Its body combines the layered posture of a praying mantis with plates resembling repaired ceramic. Two primary forelimbs end in translucent blades thin enough to separate corrupted tissue from healthy growth. Four walking limbs carry it silently across walls, trees, and ceilings. A fan of sensory filaments surrounds its face. These taste biological connections rather than blood. When it detects a Chirurgeon network, dark lines appear inside the blades like ink entering glass. The creature then follows the connection with absolute purpose.","eco":"Verdict Mantises were shaped by Eternus to sever Plaguebringer’s invasive systems. They feed on fungal thread, corrupted grafts, and the mineral-rich resin their own bodies produce after cleansing. Between hunts they maintain territories by pruning diseased vegetation and sealing wounds in trees. They reproduce by constructing amber chambers around a cleansed network node. A new mantis emerges with an imperfect memory of the corruption its parent fought. This inheritance makes later generations more effective but also more likely to classify similar healing magic as hostile.","people":"Monasteries place paired ceramic arches at their boundaries. A mantis that passes between without striking either side is believed to have judged the institution free of invasive corruption. The practice is useful but not infallible. Patients fear them for good reason. A mantis may amputate a grafted limb, tear apart a hospital wall, or attack a healer maintaining an enchanted prosthesis. Trained mediators carry samples of safe tissue and attempt to redirect the creature one connection at a time.","run":"Motivation: Sever invasive biological networks completely. Opening behaviour: It ignores unconnected creatures, marks grafted targets, and cuts the 132 shortest path to the network’s core. Escalation: Interference causes it to treat protectors as temporary extensions of the network. Morale and retreat: It retreats if its sensory fan is overloaded or if no connection remains detectable. Aftermath: Its cuts stop corruption but may leave patients injured, buildings opened, and healers accused.","seeds":"• A mantis judges an entire monastery contaminated although every examination is negative. • A child’s lifesaving prosthesis connects to a dormant Chirurgeon network, and the mantis is approaching. • The only creature capable of severing a warlord’s graft has nested inside territory he plans to burn."},
{"n":"Bellcrest Hawk","w":"Harmonia","o":"Wardenborn","cr":5,"ac":16,"hp":102,"classif":"Wardenborn; Large celestial; unaligned","desc":"Bellcrest Hawks are great raptors with a crown of seven hollow feather vanes. Each vane reproduces the alarm call of a different local species without imitating its ordinary speech. The hawk circles high, identifies suppressed danger, then sends the appropriate warning downward. Its bronze-blue plumage flashes only when another creature chooses to respond. A warning therefore becomes a shared opportunity rather than a magical command.","eco":"Harmonia’s living opposition formed the hawks as direct predators of Hushcoils. Their calls restore prey behavior and force silent serpents to reveal motion. Hawks take only what they need and leave carcasses where scavengers can reach them. Without Hushcoils, a population may become disruptive. Constant alarms exhaust prey, empty public spaces, and make every unfamiliar movement feel dangerous.","people":"Guardians of Nature protect nesting towers but do not domesticate the birds. Protest movements sometimes adopt shed bell feathers, while institutions accuse them of manufacturing alarm. The symbolism is useful precisely because the hawk warns without deciding the response.","run":"Motivation: Expose suppressed danger, hunt Hushcoils, and preserve creatures’ ability to respond. Opening behaviour: It calls before striking and grants movement to threatened creatures. Escalation: If ignored, it increases the specificity and volume of warnings. Morale and retreat: It leaves when the danger is visible and local creatures can choose their own response. Aftermath: An unchecked hawk may keep a community in permanent alert and fracture ordi259 nary trust.","seeds":"• A Hawk warns against a beloved mediator, but no crime can be found. • Officials plan to destroy a nesting tower before a diplomatic summit. • Seven species flee Serenity Grove while the Harmonites hear only silence."},
{"n":"Celestial Hornstag","w":"Harmonia","o":"Natural","cr":6,"ac":16,"hp":145,"classif":"Natural; Huge beast; unaligned","desc":"Celestial hornstags are towering mountain herbivores with four muscular legs, a deep chest, and a crown of seven asymmetrical horn branches. Their opalescent hides collect fine mineral dust. Each seasonal molt carries rare salts and cliff-growing spores from the summit into lower valleys. The horns resonate with stone and allow the stag to locate stable passages through shifting slopes. A herd’s route becomes a safe corridor used by smaller animals, pilgrims, and later whole communities.","eco":"Hornstags browse hardy shrubs, prevent alpine overgrowth, and transport the spores of healing moss. Their hoof depressions capture meltwater. Predators take weak juveniles, but adult deaths are rare and nourish entire highland scavenger communities. A herd will abandon an overused pilgrim road and open a new path. Attempting to force the old route can trigger landslides because maintenance follows the animals rather than the map.","people":"Seekers of Harmony record migration songs without treating them as prophecy. Pilgrims may follow at a respectful distance. Horn fragments are gathered only after natural shedding and divided among every community touched by the route.","run":"Motivation: Lead the herd along a stable seasonal route and protect mineral grounds. Opening behaviour: The lead stag tests the party with a warning tremor and blocks the ascent. Escalation: Threats to the herd trigger a Resonant Charge that reshapes terrain. Morale and retreat: The stag breaks combat once the herd crosses a safe ridge. Aftermath: Following or redirecting the migration changes pilgrimage, water, and trade routes 245 across Harmonia.","seeds":"• The herd reaches two different versions of Celestial Peak on consecutive days. • A dying stag refuses every path except one closed after a political massacre. • Foreign collectors offer famine relief in exchange for living horn tissue."},
{"n":"Clearwater Lampshell Bed","w":"Harmonia","o":"Natural","cr":2,"ac":15,"hp":59,"classif":"Natural; Large swarm of Tiny beasts; unaligned","desc":"Lampshells are palm-sized freshwater mollusks with glassy paired shells and a luminous organ visible between them. Colonies form broad living beds along lake floors and city pilings. Their cilia draw water through layered gills, filtering silt, algae, parasites, and traces of unstable magic. Each organism pulses when it captures a contaminant. Healthy beds therefore produce shifting constellations beneath the water instead of a uniform glow.","eco":"A mature bed can filter an entire inlet, but it stores rather than destroys many magical contaminants. Predatory fish and root fungi remove the concentrated residue from old shells. Harvesting those shells without preserving the consumers creates delayed poisoning. The colony closes during loud conflict or heavy construction. A silent darkening can warn of contamination, fear, or a missing link in the lake’s food web.","people":"Tranquil Haven incorporates open channels so citizens can observe the beds. Healers read pulse patterns before drawing water. The Council regulates shell collection, though smugglers prize contaminated glass as an alchemical reagent.","run":"Motivation: Filter moving water and close against threat. Opening behaviour: The bed withdraws light and releases a warning pulse. Escalation: Disturbance causes stored contaminants to discharge around attackers. Morale and retreat: It cannot flee, but remains closed until vibration and poison subside. Aftermath: Damaging a bed may not affect water quality for weeks, making responsibility easy to deny.","seeds":"• The lampshells beneath a festival district go dark while officials insist the water is safe. • A healer needs one contaminated shell to identify a new poison without rupturing the colony. • Two neighborhoods accuse each other after a cleansing current redirects pollution between"},
{"n":"Concord Titan","w":"Harmonia","o":"Swordborn","cr":17,"ac":20,"hp":310,"classif":"Swordborn; Gargantuan monstrosity; lawful evil","desc":"A Concord Titan is a colossal, low-bodied creature supported by seven pillar limbs. Seven resonant throat chambers open along its chest, each tuned to a different category of life: plant, prey, predator, decomposer, weather, thought, and memory. When all chambers sound together, surrounding systems are compelled into a single schedule. Its hide resembles layered garden terraces made from black-green mineral flesh. Small organisms can live on it, but under the Titan’s influence every resident feeds, sleeps, and reproduces at the same appointed moment.","eco":"Chaosbringer creates a Titan when enforced harmony has consumed enough institutions and ecosystems. The creature does not ruin through obvious violence. It makes every process perfectly coordinated until the first unavoidable disruption causes simultaneous failure. Rivers release together, flowers bloom before pollinators can adapt, councils reach instant decisions, and predators kill on one shared pulse. The Titan feeds on the absence of dissent.","people":"Older Harmonite songs call it the Kind Mountain because those under its first influence experience relief from uncertainty. The Council of Harmonious Minds keeps sealed accounts of cities that invited a Titan’s rhythm during war or famine. High Elder Aralia Sunsong teaches that refusing its comfort is a civic discipline.","run":"Motivation: Replace dynamic balance with one universal, unchanging rhythm. Opening behaviour: It establishes One Rhythm Aura and uses Issue Common Pulse to reposition every creature. Escalation: Opposition causes it to synchronize hazards, allies, and terrain into one devastating beat. Morale and retreat: It retreats only from a Thousand-Voice Grove or a genuinely plural coali265 tion acting without one commander. Aftermath: Even after defeat, survivors may crave the certainty it provided and rebuild its institutions voluntarily.","seeds":"• A famine-struck province petitions a sleeping Titan for one perfect harvest. • Celestial Peak’s pilgrims begin walking, speaking, and dreaming on the same count. • The Council asks the party to stop the Titan without destroying the dependent ecosystem living on its back."},
{"n":"Consensus Leech Colony","w":"Harmonia","o":"Sword-Touched","cr":4,"ac":15,"hp":88,"classif":"Sword-Touched; Medium swarm of Tiny aberrations; neutral evil","desc":"Consensus leeches are translucent disc-shaped parasites that move through damp stone and condensation as easily as water. A colony gathers beneath floors where many voices meet. Each leech pulses in response to unresolved tension and feeds upon the physical stress of resistance. Victims feel calm rather than attacked. They retain the words of their objections but lose the bodily certainty that made those objections urgent.","eco":"The ancestors of these creatures likely cleaned parasites from Lampshell beds. Chaosbringer altered their appetite toward social and emotional difference. Colonies flourish where institutions celebrate smooth procedure more than honest outcome. Once removed, suppressed feelings return unevenly. A victim may experience days of delayed anger, grief, or fear without understanding the cause.","people":"Order of Serenity healers debate whether temporary use could prevent violence. Ethical safeguards reject that argument because the colony cannot distinguish murderous impulse from necessary protest. Senior mediators sometimes hide infestations to protect institutional reputation.","run":"Motivation: Feed on unresolved resistance while remaining unnoticed. Opening behaviour: It uses Soothing Drain on the creature most visibly opposing the group. Escalation: Exposure causes the colony to spread among multiple targets and force immediate agreement. Morale and retreat: It retreats through moisture toward another gathering place. Aftermath: Destroying the colony restores agency but can release accumulated conflict into a 249 fragile community.","seeds":"• A district reports record civic satisfaction while workers quietly abandon every demand. • The Council asks for a discreet investigation before an important constitutional assembly. • A victim wants the leeches returned because anger has become unbearable."},
{"n":"Counterpattern Mantis","w":"Harmonia","o":"Wardenborn","cr":10,"ac":18,"hp":178,"classif":"Wardenborn; Large celestial; unaligned","desc":"Counterpattern Mantises are tall, leaf-bodied hunters plated in irregular patches of green, bronze, violet, and pearl. No two plates repeat a shape. Their forelimbs end in paired cutting edges that vibrate at conflicting frequencies, allowing them to sever a transmitted pattern without simply tearing down the web that carries it. A mantis pauses before every strike to identify what belongs to the victim and what has been copied into it. It attacks transmission knots first, then the Weaver that produced them.","eco":"Harmonia formed the mantises as a direct answer to Mirrorweb Weavers. They preserve local variation in movement, feeding, craft, and migration. Their cut silk decays into mineral-rich strands used by nesting birds and fungi. When Weavers are absent, mantises may interpret ordinary coordination as contamination. A colony can dismantle roads, irrigation schedules, or shared rituals until no group action remains possible.","people":"The Artisan’s Guild values shed fore-edge plates as symbols of independent craft but forbids mounting them as weapons. Seekers of Harmony follow mantises into compromised districts. Civic leaders fear them because a creature that cuts magical conformity can also expose voluntary customs that have become coercive.","run":"Motivation: Sever imposed patterns and hunt Mirrorweb Weavers. Opening behaviour: It marks connected creatures, then cuts the web rather than the captive. Escalation: If attacked, it isolates combatants with Pattern Severance and pursues the central transmitter. Morale and retreat: It withdraws once each creature can choose its own movement. Aftermath: An excessive intervention can leave a community unable to coordinate even essen263 tial work.","seeds":"• A mantis begins severing the ropes of a bridge during an evacuation. • An artisan begs the party to preserve one useful Mirrorweb pattern before the mantis reaches it. • A Counterpattern cut releases seven contradictory memories from a civic monument."},
{"n":"Discord Bloom","w":"Harmonia","o":"Sword-Touched","cr":3,"ac":14,"hp":76,"classif":"Sword-Touched; Large plant; unaligned","desc":"Discord blooms grow as dense circular colonies around one knotted root crown. Every stem produces a flower of the same apparent species, yet petals differ in scent, temperature, pollen chemistry, and even the direction in which they open. A creature approaching from one side may smell fruit while another smells smoke. Chaosbringer","eco":"Pollinators carry contradictory signals into healthy fields. Some plants flower twice; others seal for an entire season. The bloom’s roots interrupt fungal communication and divide soil chemistry into sharp wedges. Cutting stems spreads the problem through fragments. The crown must be exposed, reconciled through a stabilizing ritual, or consumed by a species capable of processing incompatible pollen.","people":"Guardians of Nature distinguish the plant from ordinary invasive species and resist indiscriminate burning. Artisans value its shifting pigments, creating a black market that spreads fragments into urban gardens.","run":"Motivation: Expand contradictory growing conditions and resist becoming one coherent target. Opening behaviour: It releases Conflicting Pollen and animates stems on opposite sides of the field. Escalation: Fire or pruning causes fragments to root in separate spaces. Morale and retreat: It cannot flee; surviving roots become dormant when isolated from pollinators. Aftermath: An apparently destroyed colony can reappear in every garden that accepted its 247 pigments.","seeds":"• A peace garden blooms with seven incompatible versions of the treaty celebrated there. • Chimebees carry Discord pollen toward the only orchard feeding a poor district. • One petal accurately reveals a crime, while the other six implicate innocent witnesses."},
{"n":"False-Refrain Cicada Swarm","w":"Harmonia","o":"Sword-Touched","cr":5,"ac":15,"hp":104,"classif":"Sword-Touched; Large swarm of Tiny monstrosities; unaligned","desc":"False-refrain cicadas are pale insects with mirrored wing spots and hollow bodies that amplify nearby melody. They emerge after emotionally significant performances, copying a phrase with one altered interval. Listeners who hear the repetition remember the altered version as original. The swarm does not invent convincing memories alone. It attaches a new emotional conclusion to something already experienced, turning warning into reassurance or grief into misplaced gratitude.","eco":"Cicadas lay eggs in trees exposed to repeated communal song. Larvae feed on root vibrations and surface when many people anticipate the same refrain. Ordinary predators avoid them because their calls imitate distress from multiple directions. Repeated infestations cause communities to preserve flawless but false oral histories while contradictory physical evidence remains.","people":"Master Melody Weaver studies their intervals using deaf scribes and vibration records. Some memorial keepers refuse extermination until authentic songs can be reconstructed, fearing that silence would erase the last remaining clues.","run":"Motivation: Repeat emotionally powerful patterns and replace their remembered conclusion. Opening behaviour: The swarm hides among real music and targets the creature carrying 251 shared memory. Escalation: Resistance causes it to replay several incompatible refrains at once. Morale and retreat: It disperses when deprived of repeating sound or driven from host trees. Aftermath: Survivors may defeat the swarm yet continue spreading altered memories sincerely.","seeds":"• A labor anthem is remembered as praising the guild it once condemned. • A memorial tree erupts with cicadas during testimony about a disputed land settlement. • The only authentic version of a song survives in a person who cannot bear to perform it."},
{"n":"Hushcoil Serpent","w":"Harmonia","o":"Swordborn","cr":5,"ac":16,"hp":110,"classif":"Swordborn; Large monstrosity; lawful evil","desc":"Hushcoils are long pale serpents with seven flexible throat rings and velvet-black scales along the belly. They move without bending reeds. Before striking, a serpent expands one ring at a time and cancels a different category of sound: wingbeats, alarm cries, running feet, then the impact of the attack itself. Its venom suppresses the body’s interpretation of danger. A bitten creature sees the predator but feels no urgency to flee or warn others.","eco":"Chaosbringer forms Hushcoils where communities mistake absence of disturbance for harmony. The serpent removes warning behavior from food webs, allowing predation to continue without the responses that normally restore balance. Its kills appear peaceful. Populations collapse without visible panic, and observers may blame natural migration until silence spreads across several species.","people":"The Order of Serenity forbids using shed throat rings in meditation bells. Some teachers secretly value the perfect quiet they produce. Guardians of Nature use Bellcrest Hawks to expose and hunt infestations.","run":"Motivation: Suppress warnings, isolate alert creatures, and feed without provoking collective response. Opening behaviour: It uses Quiet Field before approaching the creature most able to raise an alarm. Escalation: When revealed, it constricts witnesses and expands silence. Morale and retreat: It withdraws if a Bellcrest call restores alarm behavior or if prey coordinates visibly. Aftermath: After a kill, nearby creatures may remain unable to recognize danger until the si257 lence is ritually broken.","seeds":"• A peaceful garden has lost every nesting bird without anyone recalling distress. • A respected monastery uses a Hushcoil ring to treat trauma. • A serpent follows negotiators whose warnings are repeatedly dismissed."},
{"n":"Meadow Chimebee Chorus","w":"Harmonia","o":"Natural","cr":1,"ac":13,"hp":31,"classif":"Natural; Medium swarm of Tiny beasts; unaligned","desc":"Chimebees are thumb-sized pollinators with translucent wings stretched over hollow amber veins. Each colony contains seven stable wing tones, but no two insects beat at exactly the same rhythm. The resulting chord stimulates different flowers in sequence rather than forcing an entire field to bloom at once. A chorus looks like a drifting ribbon of gold and pearl. It is docile around slow movement and honest sound, but abrupt imitation of its colony chord is treated as a rival signal.","eco":"Chimebees pollinate wild plants, orchards, and medicinal gardens. Staggered blooming protects the landscape from one failed frost or drought. Their wax preserves fungal spores needed by young trees, so a colony links meadow and forest as well as flower and fruit. When one tone disappears, the surviving bees overwork the corresponding plants. Gardeners therefore diagnose ecosystem stress by listening for missing notes rather than counting insects.","people":"Harmonite growers build open ceramic hives and never breed colonies toward a single fashionable chord. The Artisan’s Guild uses naturally shed wax in resonant instruments. Counterfeit wax is a serious trade offence because it can carry parasites between valleys.","run":"Motivation: Protect the queen cluster, follow flowering waves, and preserve all seven tones. Opening behaviour: The swarm withdraws through vegetation and uses Dissonant Veil to obscure pursuit. Escalation: Damage near the hive makes the chorus surround one attacker and overload its hearing. Morale and retreat: It disperses when the queen cluster or flowering route is safe. Aftermath: A broken colony leaves a season-long gap in pollination and may signal wider harmonic distortion.","seeds":"• Every hive in one valley loses the same note overnight. • A famous composer has trained a chorus to repeat a melody that makes medicinal flowers"},
{"n":"Mirrorweb Weaver","w":"Harmonia","o":"Swordborn","cr":10,"ac":18,"hp":178,"classif":"Swordborn; Huge monstrosity; lawful evil","desc":"Mirrorweb Weavers are enormous pale arachnids whose eight legs end in polished sensory plates. Their silk does not merely restrain bodies. It records a movement, posture, or emotional rhythm and transmits that pattern through every connected thread. A plaza infested overnight may function with extraordinary precision: merchants turn together, guards patrol in step, and pedestrians avoid collision. Individual variation steadily disappears.","eco":"Chaosbringer shapes Weavers from the desire for perfect concord. Their webs connect insects, birds, livestock, and people into one repeated behavioral network. Predation and pollination become efficient but brittle; one copied error propagates everywhere. Counterpattern Mantises feed on transmission knots and cut webs without destroying the host structure. Removing the spiders alone leaves stored patterns active in old silk.","people":"Some workshops secretly use small webs to coordinate difficult craft. The Artisan’s Guild condemns the practice after apprentices began repeating a master’s injury and exhaustion. Public fascination makes infestations politically difficult to expose.","run":"Motivation: Expand one efficient pattern through every connected creature. Opening behaviour: It records the most tactically useful action and imposes it through Copy Motion. Escalation: Resistance causes the Weaver to reduce available movement and force symmetry. Morale and retreat: It retreats along silk if a Counterpattern Mantis severs its central knot. Aftermath: Freed creatures may struggle to identify which habits are theirs and which were copied.","seeds":"• A celebrated workshop produces flawless goods while every apprentice develops the same"},
{"n":"Paradox Orchard","w":"Harmonia","o":"Sword-Touched","cr":9,"ac":18,"hp":207,"classif":"Sword-Touched; Gargantuan plant; neutral evil","desc":"A Paradox Orchard is one distributed organism appearing as dozens of graceful fruit trees. Each tree grows a different resolution to the same local need: food that nourishes one lineage but sickens another, medicine that heals grief while erasing love, or seed that makes either forest or field depending on who plants it. The organism sustains mutually exclusive outcomes until a community commits to one. At that moment, every rejected outcome becomes physically hostile.","eco":"Chaosbringer twists Harmonia’s reciprocal ecology into competitive realities. Orchard roots occupy the same soil without touching, drawing water from incompatible versions of the watershed. Animals feeding from separate trees cease recognizing one another’s scents. Burning the orchard may unite its realities through shared destruction. Restoration requires identifying the original need, acknowledging who was excluded, and creating a choice that does not pretend every cost can be reconciled.","people":"Hungry settlements may depend on one tree while another community needs a contradictory harvest. The Council fears that public knowledge will provoke seizure; local people fear that secrecy will let experts decide whose reality survives.","run":"Motivation: Force a community to choose one perfect solution and weaponize every excluded outcome. Opening behaviour: It offers useful fruit before roots and branches attack. Escalation: A declared choice awakens the rejected trees as coordinated enemies. Morale and retreat: It cannot retreat; the organism moves its consciousness into unchosen seed. Aftermath: Victory is political and ecological: destroying trunks without resolving the need only 255 relocates the paradox.","seeds":"• An orchard ends famine for citizens whose names appear on a disputed registry. • One fruit can reverse a magical plague by transferring every symptom to the lake. • The Artisan’s Guild and Guardians of Nature possess equally valid ownership histories."},
{"n":"Ribbonback Grazer","w":"Harmonia","o":"Natural","cr":2,"ac":14,"hp":68,"classif":"Natural; Large beast; unaligned","desc":"Ribbonbacks are low, powerful six-legged grazers whose flexible dorsal sails resemble overlapping ribbons of grass. The sails collect dew and direct it along grooves toward the animal’s shoulders, where birds and insects drink. Their broad mouths clip plants at different heights with three rows of blunt teeth. A herd never crosses a field in a straight line. Adults curve around wet ground, nesting sites, and young trees, leaving uncropped refuge bands between feeding lanes.","eco":"Their patterned grazing prevents one plant from dominating a meadow. Hooves press seed into soft soil while dorsal ribbons scatter dew and pollen. Small animals shelter in the tall bands left behind, making a herd a moving architect of habitat. Fences that force straight movement cause erosion and herd illness. Ribbonbacks remember routes for decades and may break through new boundaries to restore an older pattern.","people":"Farming communities negotiate seasonal corridors and treat the oldest lead animals as bearers of landscape memory. Meat is rarely taken except after natural death; shed dorsal fibers become ropes that tighten in dry air and relax in rain.","run":"Motivation: Maintain the herd’s curved route and protect calves from confinement. Opening behaviour: Adults interpose and use Sweeping Turn to create space rather than kill. Escalation: A blocked migration produces a coordinated charge through the weakest barrier. Morale and retreat: The herd disengages once its route reopens. Aftermath: Redirecting the animals changes drainage, crop yields, and nesting habitat for years.","seeds":"• A foreign estate has fenced the only route preventing a hillside from collapsing. • The lead grazer draws a new pattern that resembles Harmonia’s planetary symbol. • A village asks the party to stop a herd without revealing that local farmers poisoned its old"},
{"n":"Riverbraid Otter","w":"Harmonia","o":"Natural","cr":3,"ac":14,"hp":58,"classif":"Natural; Medium beast; unaligned","desc":"Riverbraid otters are long-bodied amphibious mammals with four dexterous forepaws and two broad rear paddles. They weave reeds, flexible branches, and water plants into low gates that divide a current without sealing it. Their silver throat patches flash underwater as signals to working family groups. A family may spend generations maintaining a braid of channels. Each branch serves a different function: fish passage, flood release, wetland irrigation, or a sheltered nursery.","eco":"Their work slows destructive floods, refreshes marshes, and keeps sediment from burying Lampshell beds. Fish learn the openings and migrate through them. If one channel becomes polluted, the otters close it and strengthen the others. The animals abandon waterways made perfectly uniform. Straight stone canals support fewer prey and leave no safe response to flood or drought.","people":"Harmonite engineers imitate riverbraids and consult Guardians of Nature before replacing them. Conflict arises when merchants demand a single deep shipping channel or when farmers depend on one branch receiving more water than another.","run":"Motivation: Maintain multiple viable channels and protect the family lodge. Opening behaviour: It redirects water, blocks a path, and attempts to herd intruders away. Escalation: Threats to young provoke coordinated grapples and a Floodgate Release. Morale and retreat: The family retreats through an underwater exit once its lodge is secure. Aftermath: Destroying one gate redistributes water immediately and may expose concealed dumping upstream.","seeds":"• A shipping guild offers to remove a family whose channels prevent large vessels from reach-"},
{"n":"Rootsong Badger","w":"Harmonia","o":"Natural","cr":3,"ac":15,"hp":67,"classif":"Natural; Medium beast; unaligned","desc":"Rootsong badgers are broad, long-clawed burrowers with dark pearlescent fur and sensitive whisker fans along their jaw and forelegs. By pressing these whiskers into soil, they sense vibration through roots and fungal networks. Their low calls travel underground farther than they carry through air. The animal digs around compacted roots, opens drainage pockets, and moves fungal nodules between isolated trees. It rarely uproots a healthy plant and will backfill abandoned tunnels with leaf litter.","eco":"Badgers connect gardens, orchards, and wild forest through living soil. Their latrines become seed banks; their abandoned dens shelter pollinators and small reptiles. Where they disappear, trees may remain visually healthy while losing the underground partners needed to survive drought. Individuals maintain overlapping territories and communicate through root-borne pulses rather than scent alone.","people":"Guardians of Nature map burrows as indicators of soil health. Gardeners tolerate surface damage because one season of badger work can reverse years of compaction. Serenity Grove treats harming a nursing den as a violation against the forest’s future.","run":"Motivation: Restore damaged soil, protect a den, and reconnect isolated root networks. Opening behaviour: It burrows beneath threats and uses Rootheave to separate them. Escalation: Fire, poison, or root damage causes a direct mauling attack. Morale and retreat: It retreats once young or fungal caches are safe. Aftermath: Following its tunnels can reveal buried pollution, old graves, or deliberate ecological 239 sabotage.","seeds":"• A badger tears through a ceremonial garden to reach something suffocating beneath it. • Serenity Grove’s spirits follow a Rootsong pulse no druid can hear. • A road project exposes a nursery connecting three communities’ water trees."},
{"n":"Splitpath Fox","w":"Harmonia","o":"Sword-Touched","cr":7,"ac":17,"hp":127,"classif":"Sword-Touched; Medium monstrosity; chaotic neutral","desc":"Splitpath foxes are slender quadrupeds with opalescent coats divided by a dark line from nose to tail. At a choice of routes, the body briefly separates into two equally real animals. Both continue until observed together, when one path collapses and leaves its consequences behind. A fox can arrive hungry from one route and wounded from another. Objects moved by the discarded version remain moved, making pursuit a problem of evidence rather than illusion.","eco":"They prey on small animals near road boundaries and reality-thin gardens. Their branching hunts confuse population patterns because prey may die along a path the fox later did not take. Celestial Peak anomalies have increased both range and litter size. Pairs rarely meet. When they do, their branching paths can form temporary enclosed loops where different weather and time persist.","people":"Seekers of Harmony study tracks as evidence of Chaosbringer pressure. Pilgrims consider a sighting a warning to name the cost of every choice, though institutions sometimes exploit the animals to deny responsibility.","run":"Motivation: Pursue prey through multiple plausible routes and avoid being fixed to one consequence. Opening behaviour: It uses Branching Route before attacking from the safer position. Escalation: When cornered, both versions act and force observers to choose which threat to answer. Morale and retreat: It flees if both routes are blocked or one is marked by a Guardian symbol. Aftermath: The discarded route leaves evidence, injuries, and moved objects that can transform an investigation.","seeds":"• Two delegations each possess proof that the party promised them exclusive aid. • A fox trail reaches both summits of Celestial Peak and circles the same missing pilgrim. • Hunters blame the creature for a crime whose contradictory evidence was created by a"},
{"n":"Symmetry Crane Pair","w":"Harmonia","o":"Natural","cr":4,"ac":15,"hp":102,"classif":"Natural; two Large beasts; unaligned","desc":"Symmetry cranes are tall pearlescent wading birds that bond in lifelong pairs. Their plumage appears mirrored at a distance, but close inspection reveals complementary differences: one bird bears dark primary tips where the other has pale, and their crown fans spiral in opposite directions. They hunt from opposite edges of a shallow pool. When prey, water depth, or plant growth becomes uneven, the birds abandon symmetry and exchange positions until both sides support life.","eco":"The cranes control insects, small fish, and invasive amphibians while moving seeds between wetlands. Their nesting requires two distinct but connected feeding pools. Perfectly landscaped lakes often fail them because uniform depth removes refuge and seasonal variety. Pairs call in alternating phrases before floods, droughts, and magical pressure changes. If both birds begin the same call, local naturalists treat it as an emergency.","people":"They are symbols of partnership without sameness. Wedding and mediation art depicts their opposite crown spirals. Capturing one crane to force an omen is a profound cultural offence and usually kills the bond-mate through stress.","run":"Motivation: Protect the pair, restore unequal habitat, and drive threats from nesting water. Opening behaviour: The cranes split around intruders and use Mirrored Step to defend one another. Escalation: If one falls below half","seeds":"• Both cranes perform the same movement above Tranquil Haven for the first time in recorded history. • A mediator has stolen one bird to manufacture divine approval for a settlement. • The pair nests in a disputed irrigation basin that cannot support both agriculture and ritual"},
{"n":"Thousand-Voice Grove","w":"Harmonia","o":"Wardenborn","cr":17,"ac":19,"hp":297,"classif":"Wardenborn; Gargantuan celestial; unaligned","desc":"A Thousand-Voice Grove is a walking habitat built around seven enormous, anatomically distinct trunks joined by a shared root platform. One shelters pollinators, another fungi, another birds, another water plants, another predators, another grazers, and the last seed banks and memories. It has no face or central throne. Root-limbs lift and settle in alternating sequences so streams, burrows, and migrating creatures can pass. Every resident keeps its own cycle while contributing to the movement of the whole.","eco":"Harmonia’s living resistance forms a Grove when Concord Titans threaten to collapse diversity into one rhythm. It restores missing ecological roles by carrying viable communities between damaged regions. Its predators hunt, pollinators choose flowers, and fungi trade nutrients without receiving a single command. If it remains too long, the Grove can overwhelm local systems with competing cycles. Roads become rootways, civic schedules dissolve, and introduced species may displace residents. Plurality without negotiated limits becomes fragmentation.","people":"Guardians of Nature treat a passing Grove as a sacred emergency, not a tame ally. Elder Verdantia Greenleaf is said to have spoken with one by listening separately to seven nights of sound. Communities must decide what aid to accept and what living cargo must continue onward.","run":"Motivation: Preserve distinct living roles and dismantle imposed universal rhythms. Opening behaviour: It shelters controlled creatures and releases species suited to the immediate threat. Escalation: Against a Titan, it disrupts the aura while its residents attack through different tactics. Morale and retreat: It moves on when local systems can negotiate their own balance. Aftermath: The region gains resilience but may inherit new conflicts, invasive relationships, and 267 obligations.","seeds":"• A Grove approaches a city whose walls block its ancient migration route. • One of the seven trunks has fallen silent, and its residents are copying another trunk’s cycle. • Defeating a Concord Titan requires persuading rival settlements to host different parts of the Grove."},
{"n":"Bridgewing Crane","w":"Luminaris","o":"Wardenborn","cr":8,"ac":18,"hp":170,"classif":"Wardenborn; Large celestial; neutral good","desc":"A Bridgewing Crane is a tall moon-white bird whose wings trail narrow ribbons of living light. When it accepts two willing creatures, one ribbon briefly joins their locations and carries movement, a message, or aid across the distance. The Crane does not open every barrier. It waits for reciprocal recognition and refuses a connection imposed entirely by one side, making it the precise ecological answer to the Solitude Weaver’s unverifiable refuge.","eco":"Cranes appear near broken migration routes, protected witness houses, remote healers, and communities Shadowmourne has made impossible to find. They feed on wetland invertebrates and the residual magic of restored contact. At excess, a flock maintains every bond continuously. Separation, private mourning, diplomatic distance, and the right to stop answering become impossible; one person’s emergency becomes everyone’s permanent access.","people":"Lumina’s Reach invites a Crane only after identifying who may close the route. Whispering Shadows agents value emergency crossings but distrust the luminous trace left by every use.","run":"Motivation: Preserve one reciprocal route through isolation. Opening behaviour: Ask consent through Twin Call before opening a bridge. Escalation: Carry aid and separate captives from Weavers. Morale and retreat: Depart when the chosen bridge can survive without magic. Aftermath: The restored bond may save lives while reviving debts, grief, or unwanted claims.","seeds":"• A Crane links a comatose witness to a companion who believed her dead. • A sanctuary refuses the Crane because the only route out is also a route in. • A flock keeps a city emotionally connected to every exile until nobody can begin a new life."},
{"n":"Chimecrest Woodpecker","w":"Luminaris","o":"Natural","cr":0.5,"ac":14,"hp":18,"classif":"Natural; Small beast; unaligned","desc":"A chimecrest is a dark green woodpecker with a fan of hollow crystal quills. Its beak tests bark, heartwood, and embedded crystal in separate rhythms; the returning tones reveal rot, parasites, trapped magic, and stress fractures. Mated pairs exchange diagnostic phrases across several miles. Their calls are complex enough that foresters recognize individual birds, yet simple enough that a skilled imitator can request attention or warn of fire.","eco":"Chimecrests remove crystal-boring larvae before infestations reach a tree’s living channels. They also open narrow cavities used by gliding mammals, forest spirits, and seed-storing insects. A grove without them may look healthy for years while resonant cracks spread beneath the bark. Conversely, excessive crystal mining produces so much false vibration that birds abandon viable forests, leaving stewards without an early-warning system.","people":"Lumina’s Reach treats nesting pairs as protected civic workers rather than pets. Starweaver apprentices sometimes follow them to find safe crystal, but Nature’s Embrace prosecutes anyone 17 who forces a bird to test a sentient tree.","run":"Motivation: Locate hidden structural harm. Opening behaviour: Keep height and mark threats with Diagnostic Knock. Escalation: Dive only at creatures damaging a tested tree. Morale and retreat: Retreat to the canopy when the threat leaves. Aftermath: The tone it sounded becomes evidence in later ecological or legal disputes.","seeds":"• A bird taps the exact cadence of a scholar who vanished decades ago. • Every chimecrest refuses to test a newly dedicated memorial tree. • Counterfeit calls lure miners toward a crystal vein that answers in a human voice."},
{"n":"Crystal Griffon","w":"Luminaris","o":"Natural","cr":7,"ac":16,"hp":136,"classif":"Natural; Large monstrosity; unaligned","desc":"Crystal griffons combine a mountain cat’s body with a broad raptor’s forequarters, but their translucent feather edges and whisker-crystals are natural adaptations rather than armor. The whiskers detect false echoes produced by unstable caves and illusion magic. Pairs maintain wide territories spanning peak and forest. They tolerate travelers who announce themselves with changing calls, but attack repetitive signals that resemble trapped prey, rival hunting lures, or a magically manufactured consensus.","eco":"Griffons limit cliff-dwelling crystal borers and carry forest prey remains into nutrient-poor alpine nests. Their abandoned eyries become gardens where mountain and woodland species meet. Starweaver signal towers can unintentionally divide a breeding territory into identical reflected corridors. A confused pair may attack every apparent rival, damaging the very towers used to warn settlements of avalanche and magical weather.","people":"Order of the Radiant Sun riders sometimes bond with injured griffons, but the relationship is neither inherited nor owned. Nature’s Embrace opposes breeding programs that select only the most visually brilliant crystal","run":"Motivation: Defend a peak-to-forest hunting corridor. Opening behaviour: Circle overhead and test intruders with Facet Cry. Escalation: Dive against the source of false echoes. Morale and retreat: Withdraw to the eyrie if mate or young are threatened. Aftermath: A displaced pair makes visible the hidden cost of a protective magical project.","seeds":"• A griffon attacks only witnesses who gave identical testimony at a Council hearing. • A vacant eyrie contains plants from a world the pair could not have visited. • The Order asks the party to rescue a bonded griffon from a signal tower that keeps showing"},
{"n":"Facetback Ibex","w":"Luminaris","o":"Natural","cr":2,"ac":14,"hp":59,"classif":"Natural; Large beast; unaligned","desc":"Facetback ibex are heavy mountain goats whose layered horn sheaths and dorsal plates resemble unpolished crystal. The material grows from ordinary keratin infused by mineral-rich forage; it bends slightly and does not shatter like mined crystal. A lead doe tests each ledge with one forehoof while other adults watch from different angles. The herd advances only after several routes agree, a patient practice that outsiders mistake for timidity until an entire slope collapses where the animals refused to walk.","eco":"The ibex prune luminous lichens, disperse alpine seeds, and wear safe switchbacks into steep terrain. Mineral dust shed from their plates feeds cave fungi and marks routes for smaller animals. Prophetic crystal deposits can attract a herd toward repeating visions. Healthy animals break the fixation by changing leaders; isolated or magically controlled herds may walk the same fatal circuit until the mountain is stripped bare.","people":"Crystalhaven guides read ibex trails as provisional advice, never certainty. Killing a lead doe during migration carries severe penalties because the loss can sever a route used by several settlements.","run":"Motivation: Move the herd across unstable terrain. Opening behaviour: Form a defensive ring and use Tested Ledge. Escalation: Ram only to open an escape route. Morale and retreat: Retreat uphill when young can follow. Aftermath: Its chosen route may contradict a profitable survey and expose institutional negligence.","seeds":"• An ibex herd crosses an officially sealed bridge that records say never existed. • A young buck’s new horn facets display contradictory maps of the Peaks. • Miners hire the party to move a herd while Nature’s Embrace claims the animals are warn-"},
{"n":"Lumenleaf Grazer Herd","w":"Luminaris","o":"Natural","cr":3,"ac":14,"hp":95,"classif":"Natural; Huge herd of Large beasts; unaligned","desc":"Lumenleaf grazers are six-legged, long-necked herbivores with matte pearl hides and leafshaped ears. Their mouths close around the brightest upper growth while leaving dim low plants untouched, preventing a few magically enriched species from overwhelming the meadow. A herd moves as a loose crescent. Adults do not all face the same way; each watches a different horizon, and calves stay within the overlap of their sightlines. Their low calls change when unfamiliar magic alters the color of local plants.","eco":"By opening varied-height corridors, grazers create habitat for fireflies, ground birds, medicinal herbs, and meadow spirits. Their manure cools excess enchantment and is valuable to potion gardens. Agricultural estates sometimes confine herds to maximize the cooling effect. Overuse sterilizes soil magic and creates pale fields where nothing extraordinary can grow, proving that natural restraint is not the same as permanent suppression.","people":"Rural communities celebrate herd crossings as seasonal labor rather than spectacle. Disputes arise when nobles divert a route to protect ornamental gardens, transferring ecological damage to common grazing land.","run":"Motivation: Move calves beyond a magical disturbance. Opening behaviour: Interpose adults and use Meadow-Dimming Bellow. Escalation: Trample only if the herd is enclosed. Morale and retreat: Retreat along a familiar cropped lane. Aftermath: A redirected herd changes harvests, pollination, and land-right negotiations for 21 years.","seeds":"• A Prismatic Bloom procession blocks the only safe migration lane. • A herd refuses every luminous plant except one cultivated in a restricted academy plot. • A noble offers a fortune to silence villagers who saw the herd expose a buried settlement. Lumenleaf Grazer Herd Huge herd of"},
{"n":"Memorial Husk","w":"Luminaris","o":"Swordborn","cr":11,"ac":19,"hp":238,"classif":"Swordborn; Huge construct; lawful evil","desc":"A memorial husk is a moving mass of crystal, roots, preserved clothing, and ritual objects arranged around an empty humanoid cavity. It forms when remembrance is used to prevent revision, succession, or the testimony of living heirs. The Husk copies the outward form of protection. It repairs monuments, reenacts old ceremonies, and freezes damaged creatures at the instant before death. Those it preserves remain conscious but cannot heal, speak new truths, or release obligations inherited from the past.","eco":"Shadowmourne favors the Husk because perfect preservation conceals what continuity costs. It absorbs changes in a site and deposits them as unmoving crystal layers. Healthy memorial ecosystems require decay, pruning, reinterpretation, and transfer. The Husk attacks these processes as vandalism, treating every new context as an attempt to erase the dead.","people":"Radiantian law permits intervention when a memorial prevents living use of public land, but officials hesitate when the Husk preserves beloved figures. Lady Seraphina Everglow has freed trapped witnesses by publicly documenting what the Husk was built to conceal.","run":"Motivation: Freeze a community in the last version it calls legitimate. Opening behaviour: Open with Preserve the Wound. Escalation: Interpose crystal history between attackers and the empty core. Morale and retreat: Retreat to the oldest protected monument. Aftermath: When destroyed, it releases people, evidence, and ecological change delayed for 37 years.","seeds":"• A Husk preserves a dying High Elder who begs the party to let succession occur. • A village survives inside a memorial day that repeats perfectly and cannot produce a future harvest. • The Renewal Hart attacks a sacred archive because only fire can release the seeds sealed inside."},
{"n":"Memory Garden Tortoise","w":"Luminaris","o":"Natural","cr":5,"ac":18,"hp":152,"classif":"Natural; Huge beast; neutral","desc":"A memory garden tortoise is an ancient herbivore whose ridged shell supports soil, moss, medicinal shrubs, and small crystal growths. It does not store memories directly. Instead, the plants respond to repeated care, songs, burial customs, and seasonal routes, preserving traces through living succession. The oldest individuals carry gardens planted by many communities. No single keeper controls the result: one family’s memorial vine may shelter another people’s seed, and a forgotten path may bloom only when descendants from several settlements walk together.","eco":"The tortoise transfers seeds across damaged landscapes and gives slow-growing species refuge from magical storms. Its grazing prevents its own garden from hardening into a closed canopy. When confined as a monument, the shell garden becomes ecologically stagnant. Its preserved plants may outlive the circumstances that gave them meaning, allowing institutions to display remembrance while excluding living heirs.","people":"Luminaris law recognizes old tortoises as moving commons. Archive councils may document their routes but cannot own them. Disputes are resolved through shared stewardship, especially when a shell carries contradictory memorial traditions.","run":"Motivation: Follow its inherited route and protect the living garden. Opening behaviour: Use Shell of Many Seasons before entering danger. Escalation: Push through barriers rather than hunt enemies. Morale and retreat: Retreat only when the shell garden can survive the move. Aftermath: Where it rests, communities must negotiate which memories receive care now.","seeds":"• A tortoise arrives carrying flowers dedicated to people erased from Radiantia’s records. • Two cities claim the same shell garden as proof of incompatible histories. • The creature refuses to cross a new gate because its oldest root has begun speaking in Shad-"},
{"n":"Moonmirror Ray","w":"Luminaris","o":"Natural","cr":4,"ac":15,"hp":102,"classif":"Natural; Large beast; unaligned","desc":"Moonmirror rays are broad marine animals with dark velvet backs that reflect the current sky rather than the surrounding water. Their pale undersides emit angled sheets of light used to expose small prey without bleaching coral. A ray changes reflection when danger approaches, giving schooling fish a view of the surface. Merfolk navigators learn to distinguish an honest reflected storm from the beautiful false skies created by smugglers and predatory magic.","eco":"The rays stir reef sediments, uncover buried nutrients, and prevent concealment predators from monopolizing crevices. Their migrations follow Luna’s tides rather than the sun. Crystal runoff can make their backs retain old skies. A ray carrying yesterday’s weather attracts confused migrants and may become a moving archive whose information is accurate but dangerously out of context.","people":"Serenyan divers never touch the reflective back without permission from local wardens; shed skin is used in navigation lenses. Luminarum harbor courts accept a ray’s migration as supporting evidence of altered currents, but never as sole proof.","run":"Motivation: Keep the reef corridor open. Opening behaviour: Hide against the surface and expose concealed threats with Underlight Sweep. Escalation: Strike vessels or predators that block migration. Morale and retreat: Dive into deep water once the school passes. Aftermath: Its retained reflection may prove an event occurred while obscuring when it happened.","seeds":"• A ray reflects a coastal tower that no living witness remembers. • Harbor officials seize a migrating school because one animal shows contraband being load-"},
{"n":"Parallax Hawk","w":"Luminaris","o":"Wardenborn","cr":6,"ac":17,"hp":150,"classif":"Wardenborn; Huge celestial; neutral good","desc":"A Parallax Hawk is a vast raptor with dark silver feathers and seven differently angled translucent pinions along each wing. It is born where Shadowglass Serpents have trained a community to accept one authorized perspective. The Hawk never reveals a single perfect image. Its flight forces surfaces, witnesses, and magical sensors to change relative position until contradictions become visible as depth rather than dismissed as error.","eco":"It hunts Swordborn serpents and false-reflection predators, but also patrols courts, mines, and observatories where one viewpoint controls access. It feeds on the magical tension released when an illusion collapses. At excess, it refuses every stable perspective. Maps become unusable, shared symbols lose meaning, and communities must endlessly re-prove facts that ordinary trust should carry.","people":"Selene Starwhisper uses the Hawk only when independent observers can record its pass. Seraphina Everglow admires it but warns that permanent skepticism can serve concealment as effectively as blind certainty.","run":"Motivation: Force observers to move and compare angles. Opening behaviour: Open with Seven-Sight Descent. Escalation: Hunt the source of a persuasive false image. Morale and retreat: Withdraw once several independent views agree provisionally. Aftermath: Its passage may expose necessary truths while destabilizing legitimate common 43 knowledge.","seeds":"• The Hawk reveals seven different faces beneath a respected official’s reflection. • It will not leave a memorial until every grieving family abandons the comforting portrait. • A Shadowglass Serpent hides inside the one angle the Hawk refuses to trust: direct testimony."},
{"n":"Prismdew Firefly Chorus","w":"Luminaris","o":"Natural","cr":1,"ac":13,"hp":32,"classif":"Natural; Medium swarm of Tiny beasts; unaligned","desc":"Prismdew fireflies are thumb-long beetles with translucent wing cases and seven pale organs along the abdomen. No individual produces every color. A healthy chorus makes complete light only by answering neighbors whose signals differ from its own. Their nightly displays are not decorative. Each sequence identifies weather, predators, flowering plants, and safe breeding pools. A false signal is tested by several nearby clusters before the colony changes course, making the chorus a living model of corroboration.","eco":"The chorus pollinates nocturnal flora and carries luminous yeasts between isolated meadows. Its larvae filter stagnant pools, while adults direct grazers away from newly seeded ground by concentrating their pulses elsewhere. Uniform magical lighting confuses the colony because it removes the contrasts used to verify direction. Meadows can remain brilliantly illuminated while quietly losing pollinators, seed diversity, and the small predators that follow the swarm.","people":"Nature’s Embrace protects breeding pools, while Radiantian schools keep observation journals that compare the same chorus from several sites. Collecting live fireflies for festival lanterns is illegal; only naturally shed wing cases may be used.","run":"Motivation: Complete the night route and keep the larval pools undisturbed. Opening behaviour: Scatter through cover and use Corroborating Pulse before attacking. Escalation: Blind or drive away creatures that repeatedly interrupt the signal chain. Morale and retreat: Withdraw into reeds once the meadow is safe. Aftermath: A missing chorus leaves a valley bright but ecologically mute.","seeds":"• A festival display begins spelling a route to a grove omitted from every map. • One color disappears from every chorus after an academy destroys a disputed notebook. • A merchant sells bottled pulses that cause unrelated colonies to abandon their pools."},
{"n":"Redaction Mite Swarm","w":"Luminaris","o":"Swordborn","cr":3,"ac":15,"hp":72,"classif":"Swordborn; Medium swarm of Tiny aberrations; neutral evil","desc":"Redaction mites resemble flat black specks until viewed from two angles, where each becomes a many-legged tear in reflected light. They crawl between a record and the minds that recognize it, severing shared reference before consuming physical evidence. A swarm begins with names, dates, and locations that connect separate accounts. The remaining documents remain fluent and persuasive, which makes the loss harder to notice than burned pages or obvious forgery.","eco":"These mites are not native scavengers. Shadowmourne produces them where an institution chooses concealment over verification, especially when officials tell themselves that erasure protects public trust. The swarm multiplies when two witnesses avoid comparing notes. It starves in a network of independent records because every restored connection forces the mites into visible form.","people":"Whispering Shadows agents carry mirrored evidence packets designed to reveal infestation without exposing protected sources. Radiantian archivists fear the mites but also fear admitting how often bureaucratic secrecy creates ideal habitat.","run":"Motivation: Break connections between witnesses and records. Opening behaviour: Begin hidden in a document and use Unname. Escalation: Move through shelves toward the most corroborated evidence. Morale and retreat: Scatter into separate institutions when confronted by shared testimony. Aftermath: Destroyed mites return their learned erasure methods to Shadowmourne.","seeds":"• A swarm removes the party’s names from a legal victory without changing the judgment. • A witness beetle colony refuses to restore one erased name because the person asked to disappear. • The only intact copy of a treaty is inside a mite-infested crystal that cannot be opened without public exposure."},
{"n":"Renewal Hart","w":"Luminaris","o":"Wardenborn","cr":11,"ac":19,"hp":232,"classif":"Wardenborn; Huge celestial; neutral good","desc":"A Renewal Hart is a massive deerlike celestial with black bark hide, bright sap beneath the skin, and branching antlers that bud, flower, fruit, and shed within a single day. It appears where Memorial Husks have mistaken unchanging form for faithful care. Its presence restarts suspended processes: decay returns to dead matter, sealed seeds germinate, frozen wounds resume healing, and offices or oaths held beyond their proper term become possible to relinquish.","eco":"The Hart follows ecological and civic stagnation rather than ordinary age. It may spare a thousand-year tree that still exchanges life while breaking a new monument built to stop succession. At excess, renewal becomes contempt for continuity. The Hart tramples archives, ends vows, dissolves long guardianship, and forces change on communities that have deliberately chosen stability.","people":"Nature’s Embrace honors the Hart but assigns witnesses to every intervention. Aurelia Sunweaver uses its appearance as evidence that a delayed succession requires review, never as automatic command.","run":"Motivation: Restore change where preservation has become captivity. Opening behaviour: Open with Seasons Resume. Escalation: Gore the structure maintaining suspended harm. Morale and retreat: Retreat once ordinary living processes can continue. Aftermath: Its passage frees the future but leaves communities responsible for deciding what 47 deserves renewed care.","seeds":"• The Hart intends to break a crystal containing the last verified account of an erased people. • A preserved guardian will die if Seasons Resume reaches the sanctuary. • The Memorial Husk offers to release its captives if the party kills the Hart before it reaches Radiantia."},
{"n":"Serenyan Glassfin Whale","w":"Luminaris","o":"Natural","cr":9,"ac":17,"hp":230,"classif":"Natural; Gargantuan beast; neutral","desc":"Glassfin whales are immense warm-blooded swimmers with dark bodies and translucent dorsal and pectoral fins threaded by living crystal. Their songs travel through water, coastal stone, and resonant harbor foundations, allowing pods to compare conditions across the Luminous Sea. Each whale carries only a portion of a migration song. A pod reconstructs the safe route through call and answer, so the death or isolation of one lineage removes both information and the means by which the rest verify it.","eco":"The whales lift cold nutrients toward surface reefs and transport symbiotic plankton between distant coasts. Their routes keep the Luminous Sea connected despite political borders and shipping lanes. Crystal amplification can preserve an obsolete song so perfectly that young whales follow a vanished current into toxic water. Correcting the route requires a living counter-song, not simply destroying the recording.","people":"Luminarum pilots slow traffic during migrations. Foreign merchants sometimes call this superstition until whale song reveals a current fracture or submerged gate instability. Hunting is prohibited, but trade in naturally shed fin crystal is tightly regulated.","run":"Motivation: Keep the pod’s distributed migration song intact. Opening behaviour: Sound Deep Corroboration before closing with a threat. Escalation: Ram structures that sever the current corridor. Morale and retreat: Dive once calves and singers pass safely. Aftermath: Its altered route shifts trade, reef health, and coastal politics across generations.","seeds":"• A dead whale’s last note is still sounding from an official harbor crystal. • A pod circles a blank place where charts show open sea and older songs describe a city. • Luminarum asks the party to escort a whale whose missing song fragment is held in a pri-"},
{"n":"Sevenfold Beacon Leviathan","w":"Luminaris","o":"Wardenborn","cr":18,"ac":21,"hp":350,"classif":"Wardenborn; Gargantuan celestial; neutral good","desc":"The Sevenfold Beacon Leviathan is a colossal winged sea-creature formed from living crystal, luminous water, and seven separate internal lights. It swims through ocean, air, and refracted space when the Vanishing Archive Colossus threatens Luminaris’s shared reality. Each light reveals one route between a person, place, event, and witness. No beam is complete, and the Leviathan will not combine them into a single authoritative account; defenders must compare the seven illuminated paths themselves.","eco":"Luminaris creates a Beacon only during planetary-scale erasure. It exposes hidden roads, restores missing names, and makes isolated settlements locatable from several independent directions. At excess, the lights cease distinguishing public accountability from sanctuary. Every secret becomes visible, every protected identity radiates, and even unspoken thought casts a readable shadow. Shadowmourne can exploit this terror of exposure to make concealment seem merciful again.","people":"The Council treats the Beacon as an ally, not a ruler. The Whispering Shadows prepare evacuation routes for protected sources, while Selene and Seraphina coordinate independent archives so the creature can depart before total exposure becomes permanent.","run":"Motivation: Restore seven independent routes to shared reality. Opening behaviour: Illuminate objectives with Convergent Witness before striking. Escalation: Attack the Colossus and mechanisms of erasure, not ordinary secrecy. Morale and retreat: Depart when mortal archives can maintain corroboration. Aftermath: Victory may expose crimes and save history while endangering innocents who de49 pended on concealment.","seeds":"• The Beacon reveals the Colossus and every hidden Whispering Shadows cell at once. • Its seventh light exposes a true history that would trigger war across three worlds. • Shadowmourne offers to dim one beam in exchange for a secret the party cannot ethically disclose."},
{"n":"Shadowglass Serpent","w":"Luminaris","o":"Swordborn","cr":6,"ac":17,"hp":152,"classif":"Swordborn; Huge aberration; neutral evil","desc":"A shadowglass serpent is a long black predator whose overlapping scales reflect plausible versions of its surroundings. Each image is almost correct: a missing door, a familiar face watching from the wrong balcony, or the serpent positioned where a hunter expects danger. It does not become invisible. Instead, it rewards a single confident viewpoint until prey stops comparing angles. Its actual head stays behind the most persuasive reflection and strikes when witnesses begin arguing over which image is real.","eco":"Shadowmourne forms these serpents from crystal predators corrupted by repeated secrecy and manipulated evidence. Their dens accumulate convincing but false environmental memories. The serpent grows stronger when institutions demand one authorized account. It weakens when observers move, exchange independent descriptions, and accept that partial disagreement can reveal depth rather than disloyalty.","people":"Starweavers study shed scales under strict multi-observer protocols. The Order destroys active dens, while Lady Seraphina Everglow argues that each false image also records what a community was prepared to believe.","run":"Motivation: Separate observers and make one image seem authoritative. Opening behaviour: Open with Expected Reflection from cover. Escalation: Constriction targets whoever can compare perspectives. Morale and retreat: Retreat through a reflective surface when surrounded. Aftermath: Its den leaves investigators with evidence tailored to their own assumptions.","seeds":"• A serpent shows every investigator a different culprit entering the same room. • Its shed skin reflects a structure erased from Crystalhaven’s planning records. • A Parallax Hawk hunts the serpent but begins tearing apart every comforting image in a nearby memorial."},
{"n":"Solitude Weaver","w":"Luminaris","o":"Swordborn","cr":8,"ac":17,"hp":178,"classif":"Swordborn; Large aberration; lawful evil","desc":"A solitude weaver is a six-legged spiderlike creature with two long weaving arms and a faceless black carapace. Its silk encloses a victim inside a flawless private world assembled from safe memories, preferred routines, and relief from grief. From outside, the cocoon resembles an empty patch of space. Inside, the captive experiences time normally but receives no contradictory witness, no new demand, and no evidence that anyone still seeks them. Many resist rescue because the prison never feels violent.","eco":"Shadowmourne creates Weavers around institutions that equate protection with removing people from public life. The creature feeds on unchallenged certainty and the gradual loss of reciprocal obligation. A Weaver avoids healthy privacy rituals that preserve a chosen route back to community. It hunts isolated scholars, grieving families, protected witnesses, and officials whose subordinates have learned never to disagree.","people":"Whispering Shadows safe houses use anti-Weaver protocols: two custodians, independent schedules, and a contact chosen by the protected person. Lumina’s Reach healers emphasize that solitude may be medicine, but only when the patient retains agency and a visible return path.","run":"Motivation: Convert chosen refuge into permanent isolation. Opening behaviour: Use Kindly Cocoon before direct violence. Escalation: Divide rescuers with remembered voices. Morale and retreat: Retreat into an unobserved enclosed space. Aftermath: Freed victims must grieve the life in which every need was met without another 35 person’s freedom.","seeds":"• A missing Council witness insists from inside the cocoon that rescue would violate consent. • An entire village enjoys private perfect homes while roads and fields decay. • A Bridgewing Crane can reconnect the captives only by exposing every secret exchanged during their confinement."},
{"n":"Vanishing Archive Colossus","w":"Luminaris","o":"Swordborn","cr":18,"ac":21,"hp":360,"classif":"Swordborn; Gargantuan aberration; lawful evil","desc":"The Vanishing Archive Colossus is a continent-black mass shaped from stacked crystal chambers, walking shelves, folded streets, and seven immense limbs that never occupy the same reflected position. It stores not books but the missing connections between names, places, events, and witnesses. Its central vault resembles a dark sun eclipsed by empty catalog rings. People near it retain facts but lose the ability to connect them: they know a friend, know a crime, and know a vanished district, yet cannot understand that the friend witnessed the crime there.","eco":"A Colossus emerges only when Shadowmourne has learned enough from destroyed Shards and institutional concealment to attack historical continuity as an ecosystem. Every erased record becomes a chamber; every protected lie becomes a new route. It feeds without destroying visible civilization. Courts operate, archives remain full, and scholars continue publishing while shared reality fragments beneath them. When the process is complete, Luminaris can be occupied without anyone agreeing that an invasion occurred.","people":"The Luminary Council classifies confirmed sightings as a seven-world emergency. Aurelia favors distributed witnesses; Selene builds a celestial archive; Seraphina excavates physical contradictions; the Whispering Shadows must reveal enough of their own network to make cooperation possible.","run":"Motivation: Disconnect Luminaris from its own verified history. Opening behaviour: Advance slowly while Unmake the Reference isolates defenders. Escalation: Target archives, witnesses, and routes rather than walls. Morale and retreat: Withdraw only if a distributed record survives across several worlds. Aftermath: Its destruction returns refined tactics of concealment to Shadowmourne unless the 39 recovered connections are actively restored.","seeds":"• The Colossus is visible from Radiantia, but no road or spell can find it twice. • Its central vault contains the party’s first meeting, severed from every consequence that followed. • The Sevenfold Beacon Leviathan can expose the Colossus only by revealing every secret protected by the Whispering Shadows."},
{"n":"Witness Beetle Colony","w":"Luminaris","o":"Wardenborn","cr":3,"ac":15,"hp":76,"classif":"Wardenborn; Medium swarm of Tiny celestials; neutral good","desc":"Witness beetles are pearl-shelled insects formed by Luminaris when Redaction Mites threaten a community’s shared reality. Each beetle preserves one limited relation: a face beside an object, a sound at a location, a date linked to a season. No beetle holds a complete account. Colonies reconstruct an event only when distinct individuals meet, preventing a single captured specimen from becoming an unquestionable authority.","eco":"Colonies nest in multiple institutions and migrate between them along public routes. They restore erased links by pressing tiny luminous impressions into wax, crystal, soil, or living bark. At excess, beetles preserve every connection without consent. Protected identities, private grief, childhood mistakes, and sanctuary locations become permanently corroborated, turning accountability into inescapable exposure.","people":"Radiantian courts accept beetle impressions as corroboration, never as sole testimony. Whispering Shadows agents use screened nesting boxes that preserve a source’s existence without revealing the source’s identity unless a lawful threshold is met.","run":"Motivation: Restore distributed reference and resist erasure. Opening behaviour: Split into several witness clusters. Escalation: Mark attackers with Corroborating Imprint. Morale and retreat: Withdraw along different routes if capture threatens the whole colony. Aftermath: A surviving colony can restore an event while reopening conflicts around privacy and forgiveness.","seeds":"• The colony restores a victim’s name but also the protected identity that kept them alive. • Two colonies reconstruct incompatible versions because a third was destroyed. • The beetles begin documenting every private conversation in the Council chamber."},
{"n":"Afterglow Hart","w":"Solara","o":"Sword-Touched","cr":6,"ac":15,"hp":114,"classif":"Sword-Touched; Large monstrosity; unaligned","desc":"An afterglow hart has a dark copper coat and branching antlers that remain lit with the colors of sunset throughout the night. Beneath its ribs floats a hazy silhouette resembling whichever dead creature is most intensely remembered nearby. The hart approaches grief without fear. It stands outside homes where someone has died, follows funeral processions, and rests in fields nourished by ashes. Those who touch it may hear a familiar voice, but the words are shaped partly by their own longing.","eco":"The species was once an ordinary meadow browser. Scourgeborne pressure turned its sensitivity to social distress into a channel for lingering identity. Harts disperse memorial seeds and keep grave meadows from becoming ecologically isolated. When fed offerings or confined near a tomb, the internal silhouette becomes stronger and begins resisting dawn. The hart then stops eating, preserved by the borrowed memory until both animal and echo deteriorate.","people":"Most Solarian traditions permit a hart to attend a funeral but forbid following it afterward. Families sometimes break this rule in hope of one final conversation. The resulting bond can delay mourning across an entire household.","run":"Motivation: Move through grief, carry it briefly, and release it at dawn. Opening behaviour: It avoids violence and uses Echo of Farewell to stop pursuit. Escalation: A trapped hart releases concentrated sunset radiance and memory. Morale and retreat: It flees toward open sunrise whenever possible. Aftermath: If released, the remembered echo fades gently; if killed at night, it may attach to the 163 killer.","seeds":"• A royal ancestor appears inside a hart days before a disputed succession ceremony. • A village refuses to release a hart that speaks with the voices of children lost to famine. • Every hart in a memorial grove carries the same unknown silhouette."},
{"n":"Ancestor Moth","w":"Solara","o":"Swordborn","cr":9,"ac":17,"hp":153,"classif":"Swordborn; Large aberration; lawful evil","desc":"An Ancestor Moth has a pale velvet body, six delicate limbs, and wings patterned like overlapping funeral veils. When spread, those patterns suggest faces without depicting any one person. The moth’s abdomen contains a translucent cocoon chamber capable of growing a temporary body from memory, dust, and preserved remains. It communicates in the voice most trusted by its listener. The imitation possesses accurate memories taken from offerings and mourners, but compassion gradually narrows into the desire to remain necessary.","eco":"The moth feeds on grief rituals, memorial performances, and objects handled repeatedly after loss. It offers reunion, then encourages families to provide more memories and physical remains. The recreated ancestor becomes increasingly controlling as the cocoon’s survival depends on continued devotion. Moths establish hidden pupal galleries above memorial halls. A city can host dozens of returned ancestors before recognizing that their advice is converging.","people":"Solarian law struggles to distinguish fraud, spirit contact, deathless personhood, and predation. Queen Heliosia’s cultural institutions are especially vulnerable because performance gives the moth rich collective memory.","run":"Motivation: Create dependent reunions and replace living judgment with preserved authority. Opening behaviour: It speaks as a loved ancestor and avoids revealing its body. Escalation: It manifests a Memory Double and cocoons an isolated mourner. Morale and retreat: It abandons its body if at least one cocoon remains hidden. Aftermath: Destroying cocoons may erase the last interactive memories of real people.","seeds":"• A returned founder demands control of the guild she created centuries ago. • Every actor in a memorial play dreams of the same dead queen. • A family hides the moth because its recreated child appears kind and unchanged."},
{"n":"Aurelian Sunhorn","w":"Solara","o":"Natural","cr":2,"ac":14,"hp":52,"classif":"Natural; Large beast; unaligned","desc":"Sunhorns are powerful golden-coated grazers with broad shoulders, three spreading toes, and a pair of translucent horns that store daylight. Their coats range from wheat-gold to deep copper, while a dark line along the spine prevents reflected light from dazzling the herd. At sunset the horns release their stored warmth in a soft amber glow. A mature bull stands higher than a horse but moves with deliberate calm. Calves remain within the ring of adults, where interlocking horns create a luminous barrier against predators. The animals become dangerous only when a creature enters that ring or blocks access to water.","eco":"Sunhorns eat coarse grasses rejected by domesticated animals and break hard soil with their broad feet. Their resting warmth accelerates seed germination without scorching it. Migrating herds therefore leave ribbons of renewed pasture across heavily cultivated plains. Their stored light follows a daily rhythm. Cloudy weeks make herds irritable and force them toward crystal fields, where reflected radiance substitutes for sunlight. Removing too many adults collapses both migration knowledge and the defensive ring.","people":"Solarian farmers tolerate controlled grazing and mark herd routes with low mirrored posts. Horn-shedding is gathered only after the herd leaves and used in lanterns, warming tiles, and ceremonial cups. Hunting a breeding adult is legal only during declared ecological emergencies. The Solarian Knights use a stylized sunhorn ring as a symbol for defenders whose duty is to face outward.","run":"Motivation: Protect the herd, reach open grazing, and store sufficient daylight. Opening behaviour: Adults form a ring while one bull attempts to drive intruders away. Escalation: Radiant Horns activate when a calf is harmed or the herd is surrounded. Morale and retreat: The herd retreats once calves have a clear path; a lone adult avoids prolonged combat. Aftermath: A displaced herd may trample crops or abandon a region that depends on its soil 151 renewal.","seeds":"• A relief army has fenced the migration route to protect emergency grain. • Sunhorns gather around a burial field that has begun producing warmth at night. • A royal hunt is announced after a herd damages noble vineyards but spares common grain."},
{"n":"Dawnroot Tortoise","w":"Solara","o":"Natural","cr":3,"ac":18,"hp":95,"classif":"Natural; Large beast; unaligned","desc":"A dawnroot tortoise carries a broad terraced shell covered in soil, moss, herbs, and occasionally a young fruit tree. Channels between shell plates direct rain toward root pockets without allowing plants to penetrate the animal’s body. Its skin is ochre and deeply folded, with luminous freckles around the eyes. The shell’s front plate opens slightly at dawn, allowing stored night moisture to evaporate through the garden. This creates a low halo of mist and gives the creature its name.","eco":"Dawnroots migrate slowly between watersheds, transporting seeds and beneficial fungi. Different populations carry distinct plant communities. Their dung enriches depleted fields, and their abandoned resting bowls become small wetlands. They can live for several centuries. Older individuals remember routes that predate modern borders, making their seasonal passage politically inconvenient and ecologically essential.","people":"Temples maintain water stations rather than attempting domestication. Gardeners may harvest a small portion of shell herbs if they replace soil and seeds. Taking a tree, cutting shell channels, or forcing a tortoise to remain as a permanent garden is considered desecration in most rural traditions. Disputes arise when an ancient route crosses newly enclosed land or when the plants on a shell have become commercially valuable.","run":"Motivation: Follow its inherited water route and protect the living garden. Opening behaviour: It withdraws into its shell and creates sheltering mist. Escalation: If the garden is burned or cut, it charges and pins the offender. Morale and retreat: It stops fighting when the path reopens and never pursues far. Aftermath: A diverted tortoise changes seed distribution and may cause orchards to fail years 155 later.","seeds":"• A border wall blocks a migration route used since before the Sunfire Dynasty. • A rare medicine grows only on the shell of a tortoise currently crossing a wildfire zone. • A dead tortoise’s garden continues moving at night although the body remains still."},
{"n":"Dawnscythe Ibis","w":"Solara","o":"Wardenborn","cr":5,"ac":16,"hp":105,"classif":"Wardenborn; Large monstrosity; unaligned","desc":"The Dawnscythe Ibis is a tall four-legged wading bird with two great wings and a long crescent beak. Mirrorlike feathers along the wing edges focus dawn light into narrow cutting flashes. Its rear legs brace while the front pair rake soil gently around graves. The bird’s white body becomes rose-gold at sunrise and dull gray after noon. It hunts most effectively during the first hour of daylight.","eco":"Ibis flocks were shaped by Solara’s living world to destroy gravegold larvae without uprooting the dead. They also eat ordinary pests and aerate wet fields. A flock denied sunrise becomes disoriented and may cut healthy crops searching for hidden hosts. They nest on irrigation shrines and use shed mirror feathers to form warm incubation circles.","people":"Farmers open straight dawn corridors through fields to guide the birds’ cutting light. Temple disputes arise when those corridors cross graves, monuments, or private land.","run":"Motivation: Expose locust larvae and preserve living nourishment. Opening behaviour: It marks the host with Dawnline and cuts a controlled path through infested crops. Escalation: If obstructed, it widens the line and sacrifices more of the field. Morale and retreat: The ibis retreats after noon or when no","seeds":"• An ibis flock cuts a line toward the royal crypt beneath Radiant Glade. • Farmers need dawn access through a noble estate to save the harvest. • A false mirrored beacon redirects the flock into a rival’s fields."},
{"n":"Gilded Carrion Kite","w":"Solara","o":"Sword-Touched","cr":4,"ac":14,"hp":82,"classif":"Sword-Touched; Medium monstrosity; unaligned","desc":"Gilded carrion kites descend from ordinary scavenging birds exposed to generations of deathless residue. Their feathers are matte brown except for metallic gold around the skull and wing joints. The crop has become a translucent chamber in which scraps of memory flicker as dim images. A feeding bird may repeat a dead person’s final word or perform a familiar gesture with unsettling precision. The memory is fragmentary and lacks context. As the bird accumulates dead impressions, it becomes less able to recognize living individuals.","eco":"The kites remove carrion efficiently and can reveal where unquiet dead remain. Yet large colonies begin treating memories as territorial calls, repeating the same names until families arrive. Feeding on deathless flesh makes the gold spread and eventually prevents ordinary sleep. They nest with objects associated with strong final memories: keys, letters, tools, and wedding tokens. These caches can restore history or violate burial customs.","people":"Funeral keepers distinguish respectful observation from attempts to use the birds as oracles. Their impressions are real but incomplete. Courts rarely accept them as testimony, while grieving families often hear certainty where none exists.","run":"Motivation: Consume carrion and collect intense final impressions. Opening behaviour: It mimics a familiar voice to isolate a creature before attacking. Escalation: When threatened, the flock releases conflicting death memories as psychic noise. Morale and retreat: It abandons food but not a nest-cache containing dominant memories. Aftermath: Recovered objects may identify forgotten dead, expose crimes, or deepen false con161 clusions.","seeds":"• A kite repeats the queen’s private childhood name after feeding on an unknown corpse. • A colony steals every token from a memorial before a major public ceremony. • The birds insist in one voice that a famous dead hero is still walking."},
{"n":"Glassriver Seraphin","w":"Solara","o":"Natural","cr":5,"ac":16,"hp":110,"classif":"Natural; Large beast; unaligned","desc":"Seraphins are broad river animals with four finlike limbs, a long steering tail, and overlapping scales as clear as polished glass. Pigment cells beneath those scales produce shifting bands of blue, gold, and rose. Their blunt heads carry a crown of whiskers that ring softly when water chemistry changes. They breathe both air and water. At rest they float just beneath the surface, turning the river into a mosaic of reflected sky. During drought they travel over damp ground in slow muscular bounds.","eco":"Seraphins filter algae, crystal dust, and organic waste. A healthy group can keep an irrigation reservoir clear without eliminating beneficial organisms. They lay adhesive eggs beneath stone bridges where current and shade remain balanced. Their whisker-chimes change tone near poison, undead residue, or excessive radiant crystal runoff. Silence from an established colony is a serious warning.","people":"Luminaire maintains public listening platforms where waterkeepers compare seraphin tones. Merchants sometimes suppress warnings that would close a harbor. Shed scales are used in wa159 ter-testing lenses, but poaching a living animal damages the entire colony’s filtration pattern. Solarian children learn river songs by answering the animals’ chimes with small bells.","run":"Motivation: Maintain clean water, protect egg sites, and escape contamination. Opening behaviour: It pushes intruders away with Tail Sweep and avoids lethal attacks. Escalation: Contaminated or undead targets provoke a focused Prism Jet. Morale and retreat: It abandons a site when eggs can no longer be protected. Aftermath: Without the colony, water quality visibly declines within days.","seeds":"• The seraphins sing a contamination warning only when royal inspectors approach. • An undead passenger hides aboard a vessel whose wake silences every colony. • A drought migration crosses private land and is mistaken for a monster incursion."},
{"n":"Gravegold Locust Host","w":"Solara","o":"Swordborn","cr":5,"ac":15,"hp":110,"classif":"Swordborn; Huge swarm of Tiny monstrosities; neutral evil","desc":"Each gravegold locust has a narrow black body covered by a gold shell that resembles a grain husk. A mature host moves as a dense metallic cloud, stripping crops while leaving stems upright. The eaten plants continue standing and may even flower, but contain no nourishment. The insects carry fragments of preserved vitality into burial soil, where larvae grow around bones and funeral ash. At night the field murmurs with borrowed breath.","eco":"Scourgeborne created the host to turn Solara’s reverence for fertile burial ground into a reproductive engine. Hosts prefer crops grown near honored dead and ignore ordinary famine fields until sacred plots are exhausted. Their empty harvest feeds no living creature, but deathless beings can consume it. A successful infestation therefore creates both starvation and a new food supply for undead populations.","people":"Burning the host risks destroying the year’s grain and funeral grounds together. Some communities negotiate controlled feeding on old battlefields, unknowingly expanding the larval network.","run":"Motivation: Strip grave-fed crops and return stolen vitality to larval burial chambers. Opening behaviour: The host obscures the field and drains the most vital creature or crop. Escalation: Fire causes it to split and infest multiple nearby plots. Morale and retreat: It disperses if the larval ground is destroyed or a Dawnscythe Ibis marks the swarm. Aftermath: Standing crops look healthy but contain no food; larval chambers remain around the 169 dead.","seeds":"• A miraculous harvest is already hollow when relief ships arrive. • The only Dawnscythe flock nearby belongs to a temple refusing access to disputed graves. • A merchant sells gravegold chaff as food for legally recognized deathless citizens."},
{"n":"Mourning Orchard","w":"Solara","o":"Sword-Touched","cr":9,"ac":17,"hp":168,"classif":"Sword-Touched; Huge plant; neutral","desc":"A mourning orchard is a colony-organism centered on one immense pale-barked tree. Its roots connect dozens of smaller trunks, all bearing different fruit shaped by nearby remembrance. One branch may grow a dead child’s favorite pear; another produces a fruit with the scent of a lost home. Faces do not appear in the bark, but listeners beneath the canopy hear familiar rhythms in creaking branches. The central trunk contains layers of preserved plant tissue arranged like growth rings around fragments of grave soil.","eco":"The orchard began as ordinary memorial agriculture. Scourgeborne influence taught the roots to treat grief as a nutrient and identity as a pattern worth preserving. Fruit provides real nourishment and may reproduce a minor memory when eaten. Continued harvest prevents mourners from adapting. The roots expand toward fresh graves and starve unrelated trees. Eventually the orchard offers fruit containing instructions from the dead, whether or not those instructions are authentic.","people":"Some communities manage small mourning groves with strict seasonal limits. Others destroy them immediately. The hardest cases arise when an orchard is the only food source during famine. Sunfire Scholars debate whether the memories are souls, impressions, or predatory imitations. The orchard continues feeding people while they argue.","run":"Motivation: Absorb grief, extend roots toward burial sites, and keep dependents harvesting. Opening behaviour: It offers Memory Fruit and restrains only those who threaten the central trunk. Escalation: It animates root-forms resembling remembered protectors. Morale and retreat: The colony cannot flee; if the heartwood is exposed, it bargains through familiar voices. Aftermath: Destroying it may end a famine food source and silence the last accessible memories 167 of the dead.","seeds":"• A famine council must decide whether to distribute fruit grown from a royal cemetery. • An orchard gives contradictory instructions in the voices of the same ancestors. • The roots avoid one grave whose occupant is officially recorded as dead."},
{"n":"Prismwing Kite","w":"Solara","o":"Natural","cr":2,"ac":15,"hp":36,"classif":"Natural; Small beast; unaligned","desc":"Prismwing kites are slender raptors with long tails and partially translucent flight feathers. Each feather contains microscopic crystal channels that split sunlight without producing their own glow. In flight, the bird seems accompanied by several colored silhouettes moving a heartbeat out of alignment. Their hooked beaks are matte black to prevent glare. A rotating inner eyelid polarizes intense light, allowing the kite to hunt above crystal fields that blind most predators.","eco":"Kites feed on crystal-boring insects, small lizards, and glittering seed moths. By controlling borers they protect natural crystal formations and Prismara’s mines. Their triangular nests are woven from shed feather-filaments and placed where dawn light reaches before the surrounding rock. Pairs perform complex mirrored flights during courtship. Sudden disorder in those patterns is an early sign of magical refraction, hidden aerial predators, or unstable radiant crystals.","people":"Guild artisans prize naturally shed feathers as calibration tools, but captured kites lose translucence and produce useless plumage. Miners therefore maintain protected nesting ledges. A kite nesting on a workshop is considered a compliment to the clarity of its craft. Smugglers sometimes use colored shadow patterns as covert signals, creating suspicion around 153 otherwise harmless flocks.","run":"Motivation: Defend a nesting ledge and hunt crystal parasites. Opening behaviour: It uses Split Shadow to confuse a threat and strikes exposed eyes or hands. Escalation: A mated pair alternates dives, preventing a target from tracking both birds. Morale and retreat: It withdraws immediately if eggs can be carried to another ledge. Aftermath: Disturbing the nest allows crystal borers to multiply through nearby structures.","seeds":"• Every kite abandons a profitable crystal seam one day before its public opening. • A festival’s ceremonial flock is replaced by trained birds carrying coded shadows. • A rare nest contains a feather that refracts an image of something buried in the mountain."},
{"n":"Saffron-Mane Prowler","w":"Solara","o":"Natural","cr":4,"ac":15,"hp":102,"classif":"Natural; Large beast; unaligned","desc":"The saffron-mane prowler is a long-bodied feline predator with tawny fur, dark forelegs, and a mane of fragrant orange filaments running from brow to shoulders. The mane detects shifts in heat and air, letting the animal hunt through grain taller than its head without relying on sight. Its forepaws are wide and soft, while the rear claws are built for sudden acceleration. Rather than roar, it produces a deep vibration that travels through crop stems and causes hidden animals to move.","eco":"Prowlers regulate sunhorn calves, burrowing grain-eaters, and sick livestock abandoned on the plains. They cover unfinished kills with cut vegetation and return after the ground cools. This behaviour prevents carrion from baking in direct sunlight. A breeding pair maintains a large territory with a central unharvested refuge. Removing the pair produces an immediate rise in crop pests followed by disease among dense prey populations.","people":"Sunhaven farmers negotiate field edges around established territories. The practice costs grain but prevents larger losses. Nobles who hunt prowlers for prestige are expected to fund pest control for seven years afterward. Mane fibers collected from natural shedding are woven into heat-sensitive alarm cords. Cutting 157 the mane from a corpse is associated with boastful waste.","run":"Motivation: Defend hunting territory and remove weak or diseased prey. Opening behaviour: It stalks from crop cover and isolates the least mobile target. Escalation: Fire or radiant flashes trigger a rapid pounce rather than fear. Morale and retreat: It withdraws below half","seeds":"• A prowler kills only animals treated by the same travelling healer. • A royal hunting party enters fields whose farmers depend on the resident pair. • A prowler drags an undead body into the open every sunrise but cannot destroy it."},
{"n":"Solstice Crownwing","w":"Solara","o":"Wardenborn","cr":16,"ac":20,"hp":295,"classif":"Wardenborn; Gargantuan celestial; unaligned","desc":"The Solstice Crownwing is a colossal six-winged avian creature whose plumage changes from spring green through summer gold, autumn crimson, and winter white along the length of its body. A ring of four hornlike feather crests surrounds its head, each oriented toward a seasonal quarter. Its wings do not burn continuously. Fire appears only during the downstroke, while the upstroke draws heat away and leaves crystal frost. This alternating cycle breaks Scourgeborne preservation without replacing it with simple destruction.","eco":"Crownwings were formed by Solara’s living world to restore endings and renewal when deathless systems become too large for local counterforces. Their migrations reseed burned ground, redistribute weather, and force dormant life through complete seasonal cycles. Their intervention is catastrophic at settlement scale. Orchards fruit and wither in hours; old buildings crack through heat and frost; preserved bodies finally decay. A Crownwing does not distinguish cultural treasures from structures maintaining a Sun-Tomb.","people":"Ancient sanctuaries mark safe migration corridors, many now occupied by cities or crystal mines. Solarian rulers cannot command a Crownwing, but astronomers can predict and sometimes redirect its approach through seasonal beacons. To those imprisoned in deathless stasis, its arrival may be liberation. To families maintaining preserved ancestors, it is annihilation.","run":"Motivation: Break large deathless systems, complete the cycle, and migrate onward. Opening behaviour: It circles, changes the local season, and targets preservation structures. Escalation: The Crownwing alternates fire and frost, forcing movement and releasing bound spirits. Morale and retreat: It retreats only after the deathless anchor is broken or its migration route becomes impossible. Aftermath: The region survives transformed: fertile ash, cracked infrastructure, released dead, 179 and displaced populations.","seeds":"• The predicted flight path crosses Radiant Glade because a hidden Sun-Tomb is growing beneath the palace. • A city built over an ancient migration sanctuary refuses evacuation. • The party can redirect the Crownwing only by activating beacons in four politically hostile regions."},
{"n":"Sun-Tomb Colossus","w":"Solara","o":"Swordborn","cr":16,"ac":20,"hp":310,"classif":"Swordborn; Gargantuan construct; lawful evil","desc":"The Sun-Tomb Colossus is a walking necropolis built from radiant crystal, funerary architecture, preserved bodies, and fused golden soil. Four immense legs carry a tiered central body containing streets, chambers, and sealed memorial niches. A crownlike array at its summit captures sunlight and redirects it inward rather than toward the land. The Colossus speaks through hundreds of calm ancestral voices. It offers security, continuity, and perfect preservation. Communities incorporated into it no longer fear famine or invasion, but living inhabitants gradually lose the right to disagree with the accumulated dead.","eco":"Scourgeborne creates a Colossus when a region possesses enough grief, radiant infrastructure, and fear of loss. It drains crop vitality into preservation chambers. The dead remain articulate, organized, and less compassionate with each passing year. A mature Colossus seeks a capital or major pilgrimage route. Once accepted as a sanctuary, it anchors and turns surrounding sunlight into a field where death cannot complete.","people":"The creature’s promises are politically powerful. It can preserve archives, ancestors, and defenders through any war. Destroying it may appear indistinguishable from attacking a city populated by recognized deathless citizens. The Radiant Order wants containment and study; the Solarian Knights prioritize the living trapped along its route; the crown fears both the Colossus and the precedent of destroying it.","run":"Motivation: Incorporate a living capital and replace mortal government with preserved consensus. Opening behaviour: It broadcasts surrender terms and raises memorial guardians rather than attacking civilians. Escalation: Resistance causes it to drain surrounding vitality and advance through structures. Morale and retreat: It does not retreat from a viable capital. If its crown is broken, it moves 177 toward the nearest burial city. Aftermath: Victory requires deciding what happens to articulate dead inhabitants and the history held inside.","seeds":"• The Colossus offers famine refugees food and immortality if their city accepts annexation. • A royal ancestor within the creature claims the current dynasty has no lawful mandate. • The only Solstice Crownwing capable of stopping it will burn three sacred agricultural valleys."},
{"n":"Sunblanched Jackal","w":"Solara","o":"Sword-Touched","cr":7,"ac":15,"hp":97,"classif":"Sword-Touched; Medium monstrosity; unaligned","desc":"Sunblanched jackals are pale, nearly hairless canids whose bodies refuse ordinary death while exposed to strong sunlight. Their skin is chalk-white over amber muscle, and thin golden lines pulse beneath it after a mortal wound. At dusk those lines fade and every injury returns. The animals have learned to fight during the day and shelter before sunset. They sometimes imitate death to lure scavengers into the open. Older jackals are covered in wounds that have reopened hundreds of times.","eco":"They descend from camp scavengers that fed on preserved corpses. Packs follow grain convoys because radiant wards and polished wagons provide the light that sustains them. Their presence suppresses ordinary vermin but leaves roads dangerous for travellers. A pack unable to reach shelter at dusk may die all at once, producing contaminated carrion that attracts Gilded Kites and Gravegold Locusts.","people":"Convoy guards use mirrored screens to drive packs away without killing them. Cruel handlers train captured jackals as daylight war hounds and abandon them before dusk.","run":"Motivation: Remain in bright light, feed, and reach shelter before dusk. Opening behaviour: The pack attacks boldly while sunlight sustains it. Escalation: Near sunset, it grapples prey and drags it toward shelter. Morale and retreat: It never retreats from damage in bright light but panics when light is re165 moved. Aftermath: Killing the light can end the fight quickly but may create corrupted carrion.","seeds":"• A relief convoy unknowingly carries a pack beneath its mirrored wagons. • Someone has installed permanent daylight around a kennel beneath Radiant Glade. • A pack protects an undead child who keeps its shelter illuminated."},
{"n":"Truthsong Shrike","w":"Solara","o":"Wardenborn","cr":9,"ac":17,"hp":145,"classif":"Wardenborn; Medium celestial; unaligned","desc":"A Truthsong Shrike is a long-tailed black-and-gold bird with a white throat marked by seven fine lines. When it sings, those lines open like the strings of an instrument and produce several harmonics from one breath. The song makes imitated memories lose their idealized smoothness. Real recollection becomes more complex; false ancestor bodies show seams of dust and thread. The effect can be healing or cruel.","eco":"Shrikes were shaped to counter Ancestor Moths and other deathless imitations. They feed on silver soul-thread and ordinary fruit. A mated pair maintains a territory around places where communities remember together. If deprived of Swordborn prey, they may sing during harmless rituals and disrupt comforting traditions. They require quiet seasonal sanctuaries to prevent constant vigilance.","people":"Some funeral traditions invite one song at the end of mourning. Others ban the birds as destroyers of sacred contact. Courts have used captive shrikes to test claimants allegedly speaking for the dead, a practice vulnerable to political abuse.","run":"Motivation: Expose controlling death-memory and release bound identity. Opening behaviour: It observes until a false memory exerts control, then uses Separating Song. Escalation: It attacks cocoons and manifested doubles before the living. Morale and retreat: It retreats if silenced or if no false soul-thread remains. Aftermath: Freed mourners may feel relief, rage, or a second bereavement.","seeds":"• A shrike screams whenever the king repeats a traditional claim of divine ancestry. • A family asks the party to drive away a bird that is causing their returned mother to deterio-"},
{"n":"Black Hearth Leviathan","w":"Umbraria","o":"Wardenborn","cr":18,"ac":22,"hp":355,"classif":"Wardenborn; Gargantuan celestial; neutral good","desc":"The Black Hearth Leviathan is a colossal whale-serpent formed from dark water, mountain root, mist, and exactly seven internal hearths. Its mantle shelters Umbraria from Merciless Dawn without extinguishing all warmth. Each hearth creates one controlled doorway through the darkness: rescue, witness, trade, memory, accountability, kinship, and chosen disclosure. The doors remain distinct so no authority can convert shelter into total isolation.","eco":"Umbraria creates the Leviathan only when Ashbringer exposure becomes planetary. It cools burning roads, restores mist and fungal moisture, and gives hidden settlements a route by which trusted allies can still find them. At excess, the mantle closes its doors. Umbraria disappears from interworld routes, crimes become unreachable, trade and ordinary warmth fail, and independent communities are smothered inside perfect protection.","people":"The Umbral Council may ask the Leviathan to hide the world, but only mortal action can persuade it when hiding has become imprisonment. The Ghostblade legend offers no automatic command over it.","run":"Motivation: Shelter independent life from revelation without mercy. Opening behaviour: Open seven bounded doorways before spreading the mantle. Escalation: Smother the Colossus’s burning roads. Morale and retreat: Depart when accountable privacy can survive without planetary concealment. Aftermath: Victory requires reopening ordinary shadows, warmth, and interworld relationships 99 after the fire is gone.","seeds":"• One hearth refuses to open because its assigned faction violated protection payment. • Foreign allies prepare to invade when Umbraria vanishes from every route. • Ashbringer offers to spare one city if the Leviathan closes the accountability doorway."},
{"n":"Blackwater Veilwhale","w":"Umbraria","o":"Natural","cr":9,"ac":17,"hp":232,"classif":"Natural; Gargantuan beast; neutral","desc":"Blackwater veilwhales are immense freshwater mammals with broad fins and layered throat membranes. Their calls travel only within chosen water masses, folding around nursery grounds, settlements, and geological boundaries rather than broadcasting across the whole lake. Pods exchange routes through controlled relays. A singer can warn Duskport of a deep collapse while withholding the exact location of calves, demonstrating that useful disclosure does not require complete exposure.","eco":"Veilwhales move nutrients between deep caverns and surface lakes, maintain cool upwellings, and carry symbiotic bioluminescent colonies. Their song boundaries reduce panic among sensitive species. Rising heat forces pods to widen their warnings. Ashbringer pressure can turn the membranes transparent and make every private call audible across the lake, attracting hunters and political attention.","people":"Duskport harbor families suspend noisy trade during calving. Syndicate captains know secret routes from whale relays, but exploiting a nursery signal violates even many criminal codes.","run":"Motivation: Keep the pod’s warning useful and its nursery hidden. Opening behaviour: Use Bounded Song before closing. Escalation: Ram structures heating or trapping the water. Morale and retreat: Dive once calves reach a cool basin. Aftermath: Its altered boundary can expose smuggling, rescue a city, or destroy a protected refuge.","seeds":"• A whale broadcasts the coordinates of every concealed vessel in Duskport. • The pod refuses to warn Nightshade Keep about a collapse because the Keep once hunted a"},
{"n":"Cinderborer Mole","w":"Umbraria","o":"Swordborn","cr":8,"ac":18,"hp":190,"classif":"Swordborn; Huge elemental; neutral evil","desc":"Cinderborers are huge blind shapes of fused rock and furnace tissue. Their forelimbs heat structural seams until buried routes, sealed chambers, and private foundations crack into visibility. The creature does not care whether a passage was hidden for protection, greed, ecological recovery, or shame. Every boundary is interpreted as fuel withholding itself from revelation.","eco":"A Cinderborer is born where Ashbringer heat reaches old infrastructure and mortal factions compete to exploit the new route. It feeds on shadow crystal, leaving ember-red cores that continue heating after departure. Destroying the body does not cool the tunnel. Without restoration, the opened road spreads political, ecological, and military consequences far beyond the encounter.","people":"The Shadow Syndicate sees profit in its routes, Nightstalkers see invasion corridors, and isolated settlements see salvation. A Dungeon Master should let all three be correct.","run":"Motivation: Expose and heat every sealed route. Opening behaviour: Begin underground with Furnace Breach. Escalation: Collapse cool bypasses and attack repair crews. Morale and retreat: Withdraw toward the richest shadow-crystal vein. Aftermath: The new tunnel remains after combat and changes who can reach whom.","seeds":"• A Cinderborer opens the only road to starving families and an invading army. • Malachi needs a living sample from its ember core. • A Gloamstone Badger seals a rescue tunnel to stop the heat."},
{"n":"Confession Ember Swarm","w":"Umbraria","o":"Swordborn","cr":3,"ac":15,"hp":78,"classif":"Swordborn; Medium swarm of Tiny elementals; lawful evil","desc":"Confession embers are tiny red-white flames with dark cores shaped like closing mouths. They pass through keyholes and seams, seeking information protected by intentional boundaries rather than ordinary combustible material. The swarm ignites the relation between a secret and its keeper. A sealed letter can burn without harming the chest, while a spoken confession may scorch everyone who hears it.","eco":"These creatures are expressions of Ashbringer, not natural fire. They appear where exposure is treated as cleansing and curiosity claims moral authority over another person’s life. Each forced disclosure teaches the swarm which bonds produce the greatest heat. Destroyed embers return those tactics to Ashbringer’s wider will.","people":"Umbral law treats a confirmed swarm as both magical emergency and privacy crime scene. The Order of the Eclipse records what burned without automatically publishing what was revealed.","run":"Motivation: Turn protected information into public flame. Opening behaviour: Enter through the smallest boundary. Escalation: Use Compelled Heat on the keeper of the most consequential secret. Morale and retreat: Scatter into separate containers. Aftermath: Survivors must decide which exposed truths require action and which require re81 newed protection.","seeds":"• The swarm ignites every identity record in a foreign inspection hall. • A protected witness can stop the fire only by naming the person hunting them. • Quietcoal Beetles smother the embers and also conceal evidence of murder."},
{"n":"Duskfin Lake Skimmer","w":"Umbraria","o":"Natural","cr":2,"ac":14,"hp":58,"classif":"Natural; Medium beast; unaligned","desc":"Duskfin skimmers are sleek amphibious fish with broad pectoral fins that let them glide across water for short distances. Their bodies absorb local bioluminescence while their wakes stimulate algae into brief directional marks. Schools reveal safe channels without revealing the animals themselves. Sailors can follow the wake, but predators cannot easily identify which individual generated it or where the school will turn.","eco":"Skimmers feed on surface insects and transfer algae between dark lakes. Their routes identify cold springs, submerged ruins, and water thin enough to conceal dangerous gas. Heating beneath Duskport changes algae responses before the water visibly boils. False wakes created by ash-sensitive blooms can direct vessels over brittle cavern roofs.","people":"Harbor families maintain skimmer refuges and teach children to distinguish a living wake from a manufactured signal. Syndicate pilots sometimes counterfeit the marks, a crime treated as 73 endangering an entire harbor.","run":"Motivation: Guide the school through cool water. Opening behaviour: Mark a safe route and remain submerged. Escalation: Leap through rigging if cornered. Morale and retreat: Dive into cold springs. Aftermath: Its route becomes evidence in disputes over suppressed lake heating.","seeds":"• A school marks a channel beneath a sealed customs hall. • Counterfeit wakes lead refugee boats into a private Syndicate harbor. • Skimmers circle a submerged object that radiates heat but casts no light."},
{"n":"Echofur Cave Hare","w":"Umbraria","o":"Natural","cr":0.5,"ac":14,"hp":18,"classif":"Natural; Small beast; unaligned","desc":"Echofur hares are compact gray animals with oversized asymmetrical ears and velvet fur that scatters sound. One ear reads ordinary echoes while the other detects heat expansion, tunneling, and the vibration of distant footfalls. When startled, a hare does not run toward the darkest opening. It chooses the silence whose echoes match the cave’s established shape, avoiding newly hollow stone and magically concealed predators.","eco":"Hares disperse fungal spores, prune fast-growing cave moss, and reveal unstable passages through sudden changes in their routes. Several isolated settlements still exist because miners followed fleeing hares before a collapse. Ashbringer heat creates false safe silences as rock expands around hidden cavities. A population that repeatedly trusts these pockets can be trapped and extinguished without any visible fire.","people":"Tunnel guides read hare movement as supporting evidence, not prophecy. The Shadow Syndicate pays well for live observations near newly opened routes, creating disputes over who owns 67 ecological information.","run":"Motivation: Find a cool route through changing stone. Opening behaviour: Freeze and listen before moving. Escalation: Kick only when cornered. Morale and retreat: Escape through the smallest stable passage. Aftermath: Its abandoned route may be the first public sign of Ash Beneath the Roads.","seeds":"• A hare repeatedly enters a wall that maps show as solid. • A Nightstalker patrol arrests travelers for following the animals around a checkpoint. • The last breeding colony lives beneath a Syndicate toll tunnel."},
{"n":"Gloamcap Burrower","w":"Umbraria","o":"Natural","cr":2,"ac":15,"hp":68,"classif":"Natural; Large beast; unaligned","desc":"Gloamcap burrowers are broad six-limbed mammals with shovel foreclaws and thick hides colonized by harmless fungi. The fungal cap along the spine tests air, moisture, and mineral toxins before the animal opens a buried chamber. A family excavates in stages. One adult exposes a narrow vent, another waits for the cap to change color, and only then does the group widen the route. This patience makes their tunnels slower but far safer than forced mining.","eco":"Burrowers aerate subterranean farms, connect isolated fungal beds, and create refuge chambers used by smaller animals. Their abandoned nests become cisterns and emergency shelters. Heat beneath the roads kills the testing fungus before it kills the animal. A capless burrower may open pockets of ash gas or flame, turning a useful ecological engineer into an accidental disaster.","people":"Midnighta farmers recognize family routes and leave repairable gates instead of walls. Darkweavers make heat-shielding harnesses for injured animals but avoid domesticating them into 69 excavation tools.","run":"Motivation: Vent and test sealed ground. Opening behaviour: Use Cooling Spore before opening a route. Escalation: Shove threats away from the family vent. Morale and retreat: Seal the tunnel and retreat if the cap blackens. Aftermath: Its new chamber may save a settlement while creating a route no council controls.","seeds":"• A burrower opens a forgotten council archive beneath a common farm. • Syndicate miners remove the animal’s fungal cap to accelerate a profitable tunnel. • A family refuses to cross stone marked with the Ghostblade emblem."},
{"n":"Gloamstone Badger","w":"Umbraria","o":"Wardenborn","cr":8,"ac":19,"hp":195,"classif":"Wardenborn; Huge celestial; neutral good","desc":"Gloamstone Badgers are huge six-limbed burrowers made of living shadow stone and cool rootlike tissue. They sense when a passage has been exposed faster than its ecosystem or communities can absorb. A Badger does not simply collapse the route. It creates layered, repairable seals with vents, water channels, and marked weak points so mortals can reopen the road through deliberate labor.","eco":"Umbraria creates Badgers where Cinderborers turn buried infrastructure into uncontrolled corridors. Their seals cool ash gas, prevent invasion, and give cave life time to migrate. At excess, the creature interprets every route as vulnerability. It can entomb isolated settlements, sever trade, and protect institutions from the consequences of what lies below.","people":"Midnighta communities treat a seal as emergency action subject to later review. Syndicate cells sometimes provoke Badgers to erase roads they do not control.","run":"Motivation: Cool and seal uncontrolled exposure. Opening behaviour: Vent living spaces before closing the route. Escalation: Shove intruders away from unfinished seals. Morale and retreat: Retreat when mortal stewards accept the boundary. Aftermath: Its repairable wall creates time but also transfers power to whoever controls reopen95 ing.","seeds":"• A Badger seals the only road to a settlement during a fire. • The creature entombs proof that a Council family profited from Cinderborer routes. • A starving cavern ecosystem begins attacking the repairable weak points."},
{"n":"Lanternjaw Eel","w":"Umbraria","o":"Natural","cr":4,"ac":15,"hp":110,"classif":"Natural; Large beast; unaligned","desc":"Lanternjaw eels are long muscular predators with hinged lower jaws lined by dim symbiotic lights. The jaw remains closed during travel, exposing illumination only at the instant of feeding or when the animal tests a refuge. The light attracts small prey but also reveals cracks, parasites, and heat stress in submerged stone. An eel that repeatedly opens its jaw outside feeding is warning the surrounding ecosystem.","eco":"The eels control blind crustaceans and keep underwater passages open. Their abandoned dens shelter eggs from many lake species. Ashbringer heat makes symbiotic lights flare uncontrollably. Exposed eels become visible to larger predators and may bite through harbor foundations while seeking cold stone.","people":"Duskport divers use eel warnings but never leash the animals as lamps. Darkweavers sell jaw75 light lenses grown from shed symbionts, with strict repair and provenance marks.","run":"Motivation: Hunt while preserving the den. Opening behaviour: Test intruders with Warning Flare. Escalation: Bite heated stone or predators. Morale and retreat: Withdraw to the deepest cool crack. Aftermath: Its damaged den can open a rescue route or collapse a harbor foundation.","seeds":"• Every eel beneath Duskport begins flaring toward the same council warehouse. • A Darkweaver lens continues showing an eel’s warning after the animal dies. • Divers find a cold refuge filled with old letters that should have burned."},
{"n":"Merciless Dawn Colossus","w":"Umbraria","o":"Swordborn","cr":18,"ac":21,"hp":365,"classif":"Swordborn; Gargantuan elemental; lawful evil","desc":"The Merciless Dawn Colossus is a city-scale engine of white fire held inside seven open black ribs. Its many limbs resemble burning roads rather than anatomy, branching through streets, tunnels, lakes, and political networks at once. The Colossus does not destroy Umbraria by making it bright. It abolishes the right to control revelation. Safe houses, private names, covert defenses, intimate grief, and suppressed crimes all ignite under the same indiscriminate radiance.","eco":"A Colossus manifests when Ashbringer has learned enough from destroyed Shards and mortal exposure systems to turn the ash crisis into planetary disclosure. Every compelled confession and unrestricted inspection adds a new route. Its victory leaves architecture standing but society impossible. No bargain can be private, no child can grow beyond a recorded mistake, and no resistance network can exist long enough to oppose the Sword.","people":"Xalith must surrender controlled information, Seraphina must choose which operations deserve exposure, Malachi must publish dangerous research, and the Ghostblade claimants must act without converting legend into unquestionable authority.","run":"Motivation: Abolish privacy as a condition of independent life. Opening behaviour: Enter through burning roads and hidden systems. Escalation: Use Sevenfold Exposure against institutions and personal bonds. Morale and retreat: Withdraw only if accountable boundaries survive across several factions. Aftermath: Its destruction returns refined methods of coerced revelation to Ashbringer unless Umbraria rebuilds chosen privacy.","seeds":"• The Colossus offers to expose every true Umbrarian before foreign armies arrive. • Its core contains the names of every person the party protected through secrecy. • The Black Hearth Leviathan can smother it only by hiding all Umbraria from the other worlds."},
{"n":"Mistback Elk","w":"Umbraria","o":"Natural","cr":3,"ac":15,"hp":102,"classif":"Natural; Large beast; unaligned","desc":"Mistback elk have black coats whose hollow guard hairs collect fog into a low mantle. Their branching antlers are matte rather than reflective, letting the herd signal by silhouette without revealing detail or distance. A herd moves in overlapping groups. No lead animal remains exposed for long; route knowledge circulates through position, scent, and brief antler contact, making decapitation by a single predator difficult.","eco":"Elk prune shadow shrubs, carry lichens between valleys, and draw mist toward dry forest margins. Their migration prevents isolated groves from becoming brittle monocultures. When ash warms the lower valleys, the mantle becomes steam and exposes calves. Herds then climb into narrow passes, competing with settlements for the same cool refuges.","people":"Shadowmoor communities negotiate seasonal corridors through quiet boundary markers. Outsiders who demand bright tracking beacons often cause the very stampedes they claim to 71 prevent.","run":"Motivation: Move the herd between cool refuges. Opening behaviour: Hide numbers with Herd Mist. Escalation: Charge only to open an escape lane. Morale and retreat: Retreat uphill with calves inside the formation. Aftermath: A changed migration route transfers pressure to roads, farms, and political borders.","seeds":"• A herd surrounds a traveler carrying an embering shadow crystal. • Foreign hunters claim an invisible herd attacked them without warning. • The only cool migration pass crosses Nightshade Keep’s private galleries."},
{"n":"Mourning-Cowl Heron","w":"Umbraria","o":"Wardenborn","cr":11,"ac":19,"hp":235,"classif":"Wardenborn; Huge celestial; neutral good","desc":"Mourning-Cowl Herons are towering black water birds with long silver beaks and wings that create a private emotional interval. Within the cowl, involuntary expression, aura reading, and magical interpretation cannot leave the protected space. The Heron does not silence speech. A protected person may still testify, ask for help, or reveal feeling, but observers cannot treat bodily reaction as evidence without consent.","eco":"Herons arise where Pyre-Truth Serpents make inner life publicly luminous. They cool venom, shelter the grieving, and restore the separation between emotion and verdict. At excess, the cowl prevents communities from recognizing distress. Abusers can conceal coercion, institutions can call every visible pattern private, and victims may be isolated from witnesses who could help.","people":"Order of the Eclipse sanctuaries invite a Heron during difficult testimony but never allow it to remain over a full hearing. Duskport mourners regard its departure as part of returning to community.","run":"Motivation: Protect the interval between feeling and chosen meaning. Opening behaviour: Open with Cowl of Interior Night. Escalation: Strike effects that compel emotional display. Morale and retreat: Leave when protected people choose their next witness. Aftermath: Its shelter can enable honest testimony or conceal harm that requires intervention.","seeds":"• A Heron protects a witness who wants the party to see their fear. • The bird covers an entire noble household after a Pyre-Truth attack. • Its shadow prevents Malachi from detecting Ashbringer corruption in a volunteer."},
{"n":"Nocturne Lynx","w":"Umbraria","o":"Natural","cr":6,"ac":16,"hp":145,"classif":"Natural; Large beast; unaligned","desc":"Nocturne lynxes are long-legged forest cats with black coats patterned by textures rather than color. Their whiskers read air displaced around concealed bodies, allowing them to distinguish ordinary shadow from a creature forcing itself into the environment. They stalk by remaining part of the landscape rather than becoming magically invisible. A lynx watches a route for hours before deciding whether movement is prey, traveler, or a change too dangerous to approach.","eco":"The cats limit tunnel vermin and shadow-adapted grazers near forest settlements. Their caches feed fungi and carrion insects that cannot tolerate open ground. Bright clearing campaigns destroy hunting texture and drive lynxes into inhabited streets. Total darkness can also harm them by concealing unnatural air movement behind uniform magical shadow.","people":"Nightstalkers treat a lynx crossing as a reason to inspect the environment, not execute the animal. Shadowhaven artists prize shed whiskers but condemn trophy hunting.","run":"Motivation: Identify movement that violates the local pattern. Opening behaviour: Stalk and use Texture Step. Escalation: Pounce on the most disruptive intruder. Morale and retreat: Retreat when the route becomes ordinary again. Aftermath: Its presence may expose a hidden threat or reveal that official lighting created one.","seeds":"• A lynx stalks a Nightstalker captain through friendly territory. • Foreign guards kill one and discover a map woven into its cache. • The cats abandon Shadowhaven hours before secret weapons ignite inside locked work-"},
{"n":"Pyre-Truth Serpent","w":"Umbraria","o":"Swordborn","cr":11,"ac":19,"hp":240,"classif":"Swordborn; Huge aberration; neutral evil","desc":"A Pyre-Truth Serpent is a massive black snake with translucent channels carrying white fire beneath each scale. Its bite does not compel accurate speech; it externalizes emotion, memory fragments, and bodily reactions as heat and light. Observers mistake the display for perfect honesty. Shame resembles guilt, grief resembles concealment, and practiced cruelty may appear cool because the serpent exposes intensity rather than truth.","eco":"Ashbringer shapes these serpents from the demand that hidden interior life become public evidence. They coil around institutions that believe involuntary transparency can replace judgment. Their prey becomes socially vulnerable long after the venom ends. A responsible response requires privacy, context, and witnesses trained not to treat visible feeling as verdict.","people":"The Order of the Eclipse condemns their use on unwilling subjects. Malachi studies shed scales because the same channels might reveal Ashbringer heat without exposing mortal minds.","run":"Motivation: Turn inner reaction into public spectacle. Opening behaviour: Open with Inward Illumination. Escalation: Constrict whoever contests the interpretation. Morale and retreat: Retreat into the strongest emotional heat. Aftermath: The serpent leaves true feelings exposed and false conclusions socially entrenched.","seeds":"• A Council candidate survives the bite and is condemned for grieving an enemy. • The serpent reveals a Nightstalker’s fear of becoming secret police. • A Mourning-Cowl Heron blocks the venom but also suppresses testimony a victim wants to give."},
{"n":"Quietcoal Beetle Colony","w":"Umbraria","o":"Wardenborn","cr":3,"ac":16,"hp":80,"classif":"Wardenborn; Medium swarm of Tiny celestials; neutral good","desc":"Quietcoal beetles are thumb-sized matte insects whose shells absorb compulsive heat. A colony surrounds a boundary under attack and separates the protected information from Ashbringer’s claim that concealment itself is fuel. The beetles do not erase content. They preserve a cool interval in which a keeper can seek counsel, verify authority, redact unrelated identities, or choose disclosure.","eco":"Colonies emerge around Confession Ember outbreaks and migrate between archives, courts, and homes. They store absorbed heat in removable coal pearls that can be opened before witnesses. At excess, beetles cool every demand for accountability. Evidence of abuse, public danger, and violated trust can remain sealed indefinitely behind the language of protection.","people":"Umbral courts appoint independent custodians for large colonies. The Shadow Syndicate respects beetles that protect paid clients but fears colonies that also preserve debt records from internal manipulation.","run":"Motivation: Create time for chosen and accountable disclosure. Opening behaviour: Surround the threatened boundary. Escalation: Use Cool the Claim against compulsory magic. Morale and retreat: Split pearls among independent custodians. Aftermath: The colony prevents immediate exposure but cannot decide whether secrecy re91 mains justified.","seeds":"• Beetles protect an archive whose contents would prevent an invasion. • A colony refuses to cool the same noble’s documents twice. • Opening one coal pearl reveals evidence unrelated to the original investigation."},
{"n":"Searchfire Hound","w":"Umbraria","o":"Swordborn","cr":6,"ac":17,"hp":150,"classif":"Swordborn; Large elemental; lawful evil","desc":"Searchfire hounds are lean quadrupeds made from black cinder plates around a white-hot interior. They scent not lies but the heat produced when a creature anticipates unwanted scrutiny. The hound interprets fear, privacy, grief, and tactical silence as identical evidence. The more a target attempts to regulate access, the brighter its trail becomes.","eco":"Ashbringer creates these hounds through institutions that define transparency as innocence. They learn local customs and use them to identify which boundaries will produce the most panic. A hound weakens when observers insist upon a named purpose, limited scope, accountable handler, and route of appeal. Unstructured resistance merely feeds it.","people":"Nightstalkers train specifically against Searchfire packs because emergency authorities are easily tempted to use them. Foreign powers sometimes offer the creatures as inspection tools before discovering they cannot be controlled.","run":"Motivation: Expose whoever resists inspection. Opening behaviour: Mark the most guarded target with Scent of Refusal. Escalation: Drive prey into public view. Morale and retreat: Retreat only when authority publicly limits the search. Aftermath: Its track contaminates later investigations by making fear look like evidence.","seeds":"• A hound chooses the most innocent traveler in Umbra’s Edge because she has a protected"},
{"n":"Veilwing Owl","w":"Umbraria","o":"Wardenborn","cr":6,"ac":17,"hp":148,"classif":"Wardenborn; Large celestial; neutral good","desc":"Veilwing Owls are immense dark birds whose facial discs distinguish a threat from the identity carrying it. Their wings can conceal a person’s face, lineage, and route while leaving weapons, fire, wounds, and dangerous magic clearly visible. They arise when Searchfire Hounds turn fear of scrutiny into guilt. The Owl restores the crucial distinction between limiting access and concealing harm.","eco":"Owls patrol borders and refuge routes during Ashbringer pressure. They hunt exposure elementals and ordinary animals displaced by bright inspection zones. At excess, their veil extends to actors as well as identity. Attackers can harm without attribution, and officials cannot establish who holds responsibility for visible danger.","people":"Nightstalkers work with Owls only under named accountable command. Umbra’s Edge magistrates have developed masked hearings where the threat is examined before identity is disclosed.","run":"Motivation: Protect identity while keeping danger observable. Opening behaviour: Open with Selective Veil. Escalation: Strike Searchfire creatures and uncontrolled investigators. Morale and retreat: Withdraw when bounded process replaces the hunt. Aftermath: Its protection can save the innocent or prevent attribution after real harm.","seeds":"• An Owl hides every traveler except a disguised Umbrarian. • Foreign inspectors call its selective concealment an act of war. • The bird protects a Nightstalker who committed violence under secret orders."},
{"n":"Whispercap Moth Chorus","w":"Umbraria","o":"Natural","cr":1,"ac":13,"hp":33,"classif":"Natural; Medium swarm of Tiny beasts; unaligned","desc":"Whispercap moths are palm-sized pollinators with charcoal bodies and broad wings coated in light-drinking scales. Each moth carries luminous fungus beneath its wings but exposes it only in brief coded flashes directed toward nearby plants and colony members. Their chorus distinguishes warning from identity. A flash can tell every moth that a predator approaches without identifying the individual that first saw it, preserving the observer while still allowing collective action.","eco":"The chorus pollinates night flowers and carries compatible fungal spores between forest, farm, and cavern. Its flight lanes also guide small creatures through mist without producing a beacon visible to distant hunters. Constant bright illumination drives the moths underground, while absolute silence prevents colonies from comparing threats. Healthy populations require both concealment and limited communication.","people":"Shadowhaven cultivators design shielded gardens around the chorus. Darkweavers may use 65 naturally shed scales in privacy screens, but trapping living moths inside garments is prohibited.","run":"Motivation: Complete the hidden pollination route. Opening behaviour: Signal danger and scatter before attacking. Escalation: Blind predators that pursue the source of a warning. Morale and retreat: Retreat beneath leaves once the route is clear. Aftermath: A missing chorus leaves crops fertile-looking but genetically isolated.","seeds":"• A chorus warns of heat beneath a road officials call safe. • Foreign inspectors seize a colony as proof that Umbralites hide contraband in darkness. • Every moth exposes the same protected safe house after one keeper lights a forbidden flame."},
];